275
00:11:12.837 --> 00:11:13.233
Wow.

276
00:11:13.233 --> 00:11:14.814
A hundred and thirteen.

277
00:11:14.814 --> 00:11:16.000
That’s wild.

278
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:17.853
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yep she was a hundred and thirteen.

279
00:11:17.853 --> 00:11:19.441
She was the mother of the

280
00:11:19.441 --> 00:11:22.353
<c.p>church, and baby trust me, you had to go to church.

281
00:11:22.353 --> 00:11:23.412
<v ARSENEAULT>What church was it?

282
00:11:23.412 --> 00:11:25.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Philadelphia of God and Christ.

283
00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:26.055
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was that?

284
00:11:26.055 --> 00:11:29.925
<v HUNTSBERRY>That was in . . . Like you’re going to Westlake at the time.

285
00:11:29.925 --> 00:11:32.036
It was on Old Spanish Trail.

286
00:11:32.036 --> 00:11:37.665
Now they done moved it right in the area behind Prince Street, back up in there.

287
00:11:37.665 --> 00:11:41.183
They moved it after they . . . The plants had bought that.

288
00:11:41.183 --> 00:11:43.294
So they moved it, the church.

289
00:11:43.294 --> 00:11:46.812
But yeah she was a little wise little lady there.

290
00:11:46.812 --> 00:11:49.274
She was the mother of the church.

291
00:11:49.274 --> 00:11:51.737
Church didn’t start until she got there.

292
00:11:51.737 --> 00:11:52.792
It was amazing.

293
00:11:52.792 --> 00:11:55.255
<v ARSENEAULT>What is the mother of the church?

294
00:11:55.255 --> 00:12:01.587
<v HUNTSBERRY>They call them mothers of the church, the elderly lady, whoever’s the oldest, they would call them mothers.

295
00:12:01.587 --> 00:12:05.457
It’s just like . . . It’s just a respect they had for them.

296
00:12:05.457 --> 00:12:10.734
And even the pastor, he would sit in the pulpit and he won’t even move.

297
00:12:10.734 --> 00:12:11.789
Church was quiet.

298
00:12:11.789 --> 00:12:14.956
He won’t even say anything until she got there.

299
00:12:14.956 --> 00:12:17.770
And when she got there, then church started.

300
00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:23.399
It’s amazing how we’ll . . . my daddy would drive up and when she gets out that car,

301
00:12:23.399 --> 00:12:28.676
she’ll take that old tambourine and shake it and everybody stands up in the church.

302
00:12:28.676 --> 00:12:31.139
Everybody stand up and they start singing.

303
00:12:31.139 --> 00:12:36.416
And we had to sit on . . . Children, we had to sit on the front row.

304
00:12:36.416 --> 00:12:37.471
There wasn’t no

305
00:12:37.471 --> 00:12:39.582
<c.p>back rows like they do now.

306
00:12:39.582 --> 00:12:42.396
We had to sit on the front row.

307
00:12:42.396 --> 00:12:48.729
And it’s amazing how . . . I told my brother at how the old sisters were dressed all in white.

308
00:12:48.729 --> 00:12:51.895
They dressed in white and that’s what they did.

309
00:12:51.895 --> 00:12:54.709
But she was a little spunky little lady.

310
00:12:54.709 --> 00:12:58.931
She was spunky and she had wind up having . . . which the family,

311
00:12:58.931 --> 00:13:04.911
some of the family didn’t know who . . . and I laugh at them about me doing the research.

312
00:13:04.911 --> 00:13:08.429
She wind up having eight kids, but only four lived.

313
00:13:08.429 --> 00:13:10.892
And four of them died as childbirth.

314
00:13:10.892 --> 00:13:16.873
And when I told them that they said, “Oh, no.” I said, “Black and white don’t lie.”

315
00:13:16.873 --> 00:13:18.280
So we just laugh.

316
00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:19.687
We laughed about that.

317
00:13:19.687 --> 00:13:22.501
But like I said she was a midwife,

318
00:13:22.501 --> 00:13:28.130
and like I say she got married . . . in June of 1884 to Ethan at that time.

319
00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:32.000
And Ethan died . . . Her husband died January the twenty-fourth, 1925.

320
00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:36.297
<v ARSENEAULT>And she lives . . . Out lived him by so many years.

321
00:13:36.297 --> 00:13:39.305
<v HUNTSBERRY>So many years, yep she sure did.

322
00:13:39.305 --> 00:13:40.594
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she remarry?

323
00:13:40.594 --> 00:13:42.312
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, she never did.

324
00:13:42.312 --> 00:13:43.602
She never did.

325
00:13:43.602 --> 00:13:45.320
No, she never did.

326
00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:47.898
No that’s who she was with.

327
00:13:47.898 --> 00:13:57.781
See, her and my grandfather, they stayed together and her daughter which was Easter, they all stayed in that house on Prince Street.

328
00:13:57.781 --> 00:14:02.938
They still all stayed there and that’s where we’ll go hang at.

329
00:14:02.938 --> 00:14:05.086
That’s where we went, so.

330
00:14:05.086 --> 00:14:13.680
And see, Edison when he . . . Edison who was my grandfather, who was her son, like I say he married twice.

331
00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:20.984
He married [Alma Verdine?], and then after she died, she passed away, he married [Johnnie Hartman?], so.

332
00:14:20.984 --> 00:14:25.711
But [Johnnie?] left him too, because she died after him, too.

333
00:14:25.711 --> 00:14:27.000
She died . . .

334
00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:28.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Her name was Johnnie?

335
00:14:28.333 --> 00:14:30.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>We called her Johnnie.

336
00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:30.469
<v ARSENEAULT>Johnnie.

337
00:14:30.469 --> 00:14:31.406
<v HUNTSBERRY>Johnnie [agrees].

338
00:14:31.406 --> 00:14:39.375
So we had some good memories of them and my mom working and running the cafes and . . .

339
00:14:39.375 --> 00:14:41.719
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your mom’s name?

340
00:14:41.719 --> 00:14:44.062
<v HUNTSBERRY>My mom was Mary Louise.

341
00:14:44.062 --> 00:14:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Catlon?

342
00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:50.395
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Disagrees] My momma . . . My mother married my daddy who would be Edison’s son.

343
00:14:50.395 --> 00:14:54.960
My daddy is . . . Was a Mayo, my momma was an [Achan?].

344
00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:59.941
She was an [Achan?] so they came . . . That like I said they . . .

345
00:14:59.941 --> 00:15:02.846
<v ARSENEAULT>Was she related to the Reverend [Achan?]?

346
00:15:02.846 --> 00:15:03.261
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Agrees].

347
00:15:03.261 --> 00:15:05.336
Reverend [Achan?] . . . You know him?

348
00:15:05.336 --> 00:15:05.751
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

349
00:15:05.751 --> 00:15:07.826
<v HUNTSBERRY>Reverend [Achan?] was her [godchild?].

350
00:15:07.826 --> 00:15:11.561
Reverend Achan’s daddy and her was sisters and brothers.

351
00:15:11.561 --> 00:15:22.352
So they got married and she used to do a lot of . . . café work all down there on Highway 90 where all the cafés and stuff.

352
00:15:22.352 --> 00:15:31.067
She did a lot of that work down there, and washed clothes, picked blueberries, canned every . . . Girl talk about the canning.

353
00:15:31.067 --> 00:15:33.972
I could tell you about the canning.

354
00:15:33.972 --> 00:15:36.047
We canned everything we had.

355
00:15:36.047 --> 00:15:39.368
We canned figs, we did figs, pears, peaches.

356
00:15:39.368 --> 00:15:43.103
She did okra, she did . . . What else she did?

357
00:15:43.103 --> 00:15:45.593
She did something else . . . Mayhaw jelly.

358
00:15:45.593 --> 00:15:47.253
She made mayhaw jelly.

359
00:15:47.253 --> 00:15:49.743
We made our own ice cream.

360
00:15:49.743 --> 00:15:57.628
That’s why I say we lived off the land, so . . . and we made stuff and we wasn’t as sick.

361
00:15:57.628 --> 00:15:59.289
Wasn’t as sick, so.

362
00:15:59.289 --> 00:16:02.609
We did good versus now; everything now, so.

363
00:16:02.609 --> 00:16:09.249
And then like I said my brother, he remember a lot too, but it was good.

364
00:16:09.249 --> 00:16:11.324
It was good coming up.

365
00:16:11.324 --> 00:16:14.644
We had some good days on this street.

366
00:16:14.644 --> 00:16:20.040
It’s so sad to go down there now and see how it is.

367
00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:21.285
It’s very sad.

368
00:16:21.285 --> 00:16:24.605
But we had some good time in Mossville.

369
00:16:24.605 --> 00:16:25.435
Yeah, so.

370
00:16:25.435 --> 00:16:28.340
So what else you want to know?

371
00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:30.000
About the school?

372
00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.245
<v ARSENEAULT>Let’s see, so your grandparents, they lived off the land.

373
00:16:34.245 --> 00:16:35.943
Do they work anywhere?

374
00:16:35.943 --> 00:16:37.642
<v HUNTSBERRY>My grandfather was Edison,

375
00:16:37.642 --> 00:16:45.708
he worked . . . called back then . . . it was called . . . He was a laborer and he worked at . . . it was Lockport.

376
00:16:45.708 --> 00:16:52.500
It was [Moore?] Company turned out to be after they changed the name it was Firestone.

377
00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:56.745
So that’s what he retired from Firestone, and . . . Firestone Plant.

378
00:16:56.745 --> 00:17:00.142
He worked back there and he did that.

379
00:17:00.142 --> 00:17:09.906
And they all . . . Matter of fact, they all worked at Lockport, all the mens back there, back in them days because as you

380
00:17:09.906 --> 00:17:14.151
<c.p>do the research you’ll see that they worked at Lockport.

381
00:17:14.151 --> 00:17:15.000
And . . .

382
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.278
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of plant was that?

383
00:17:17.278 --> 00:17:25.253
<v HUNTSBERRY>I guess a refinery because if you look at the old pictures that’s what it . . . Most of it look like, so.

384
00:17:25.253 --> 00:17:26.772
And they did that.

385
00:17:26.772 --> 00:17:31.329
It was right there by the railroad track, back up in there.

386
00:17:31.329 --> 00:17:33.228
And that’s what they did.

387
00:17:33.228 --> 00:17:35.886
They did that kind of work, so.

388
00:17:35.886 --> 00:17:40.063
And like I say grandma, I don’t remember her working anywhere.

389
00:17:40.063 --> 00:17:45.000
I just remember her home cooking and taking care of us, so.

390
00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:46.320
<v ARSENEAULT>What did she cook?

391
00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:46.981
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord.

392
00:17:46.981 --> 00:17:47.311
Everything.

393
00:17:47.311 --> 00:17:49.291
Honey you had to eat, though.

394
00:17:49.291 --> 00:17:53.252
Didn’t matter if you don’t like it, you had to eat it.

395
00:17:53.252 --> 00:17:58.204
And like I said she cooks them vegetables, them green vegetables and squash and stuff.

396
00:17:58.204 --> 00:18:01.835
I hate that stuff, but honey you had to eat it.

397
00:18:01.835 --> 00:18:06.786
If you was there . . . and I used to press it, “Momma please come get me.

398
00:18:06.786 --> 00:18:07.777
Come get me.”

399
00:18:07.777 --> 00:18:13.718
So they believe in cooking all of that stuff and I didn’t care for it as a child.

400
00:18:13.718 --> 00:18:17.019
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she have like a dish that you did like?

401
00:18:17.019 --> 00:18:19.330
Something . . . Home cooking that was your favorite?

402
00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:23.291
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, not unless I can remember because I wasn’t a big eater.

403
00:18:23.291 --> 00:18:28.243
And even today I’m still not a big eater and they can’t believe the weight.

404
00:18:28.243 --> 00:18:29.563
I say that’s generic.

405
00:18:29.563 --> 00:18:31.874
And so I just laugh at them.

406
00:18:31.874 --> 00:18:37.155
They’ll laugh at me because I eat out a small bowl, but . . . and I don’t know.

407
00:18:37.155 --> 00:18:37.485
But

408
00:18:37.485 --> 00:18:39.796
<c.p>no, I don’t have a special dish.

409
00:18:39.796 --> 00:18:46.398
The things that I really liked that my mom cooked was . . . is always . . . And I guess I’m a sweet freak.

410
00:18:46.398 --> 00:18:50.689
She always had that tea cake smelling when you come home from school.

411
00:18:50.689 --> 00:18:51.680
So it was . . .

412
00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She cooked it?

413
00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:53.557
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord.

414
00:18:53.557 --> 00:18:56.620
She always had something baked for us coming home from school.

415
00:18:56.620 --> 00:18:58.013
We always had something sweet.

416
00:18:58.013 --> 00:19:03.582
And we laughed about that because we . . . we’ll laugh at that now and I’ll tell the grandkids and they say,

417
00:19:03.582 --> 00:19:06.367
“Yeah Maw maw used to do that for us, too.”

418
00:19:06.367 --> 00:19:09.430
I say, “But yeah, okay y’all aren’t getting it from me.”

419
00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:12.215
So, but yeah she used to do all of that.

420
00:19:12.215 --> 00:19:13.329
So we used to . . .

421
00:19:13.329 --> 00:19:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What is a tea cake?

422
00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:17.184
<v HUNTSBERRY>Tea cake is like a sweet cake.

423
00:19:17.184 --> 00:19:22.801
It’s a cake and it’s a . . . How you can make like the tea cookies and stuff like that,

424
00:19:22.801 --> 00:19:26.858
but you just put it in a pan and make like a cake.

425
00:19:26.858 --> 00:19:30.915
Or she’ll make a peach cobbler or something . . . It always was something sweet.

426
00:19:30.915 --> 00:19:34.972
You always had something sweet when you got off the bus, always did.

427
00:19:34.972 --> 00:19:35.596
Always did.

428
00:19:35.596 --> 00:19:41.525
But you had that big pot of beans on that stove and that chicken, but you had something sweet.

429
00:19:41.525 --> 00:19:43.397
And they believed in that, so.

430
00:19:43.397 --> 00:19:44.645
They believe in that.

431
00:19:44.645 --> 00:19:48.078
And like I was . . . me and my brother was talking today,

432
00:19:48.078 --> 00:19:55.879
one thing about . . . We call them “mothers of the street” in Mossville on Prater Road where they . . . like my daddy worked at Lake Charles Stevedore.

433
00:19:55.879 --> 00:19:57.440
He had the year but . . .

434
00:19:57.440 --> 00:19:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He was a stevedore?

435
00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:01.545
<v HUNTSBERRY>He worked at Lake Charles Stevedore, my dad Henry.

436
00:20:01.545 --> 00:20:02.676
Henry is my dad.

437
00:20:02.676 --> 00:20:05.503
He worked at Lake Charles Stevedore and retired from there.

438
00:20:05.503 --> 00:20:12.007
But he had the year, but he didn’t have the age because he had been working there since he was a little boy.

439
00:20:12.007 --> 00:20:15.683
And they would bring in like flour, and rice, and stuff like that.

440
00:20:15.683 --> 00:20:19.641
And if he brings that in, then my neighbors and them . . . Momma would share.

441
00:20:19.641 --> 00:20:22.752
And it’s amazing how we call them “mothers of the street.”

442
00:20:22.752 --> 00:20:24.448
They would share with one another.

443
00:20:24.448 --> 00:20:28.407
And if my neighbors butchered a pig, everybody got a piece of the meat.

444
00:20:28.407 --> 00:20:29.538
You didn’t hear nothing.

445
00:20:29.538 --> 00:20:32.366
Ain’t nobody talked about nobody, and that’s how they lived.

446
00:20:32.366 --> 00:20:36.041
Because they all had big kids at the time . . . Large amount of kids.

447
00:20:36.041 --> 00:20:40.848
We . . . It was ten of us, and I think Mrs. Eula had seven or eight of them,

448
00:20:40.848 --> 00:20:44.524
Mrs. [Maude?] had like almost ten kids. and I’m going to tell you,

449
00:20:44.524 --> 00:20:47.917
back then and there people had big kids . . . a lot of kids.

450
00:20:47.917 --> 00:20:50.179
And so everybody took care of each other.

451
00:20:50.179 --> 00:20:53.290
And they took . . . and them womens never talked about each other.

452
00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:54.703
You never heard a word.

453
00:20:54.703 --> 00:20:58.097
And she’ll tell me, “Go take that down there to Mrs. Agnes.

454
00:20:58.097 --> 00:20:59.510
Go give that to her.”

455
00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:02.055
Or either [Terrell?] would come bring something to Momma.

456
00:21:02.055 --> 00:21:05.166
But you’ll never hear them say nothing bad about each other.

457
00:21:05.166 --> 00:21:06.297
That’s how they lived.

458
00:21:06.297 --> 00:21:07.993
They took care of each other.

459
00:21:07.993 --> 00:21:09.972
And that’s why I told the kids,

460
00:21:09.972 --> 00:21:15.628
I say now if I tell you go next door and get some rice you’re going to hear about it.

461
00:21:15.628 --> 00:21:17.041
But they didn’t do that.

462
00:21:17.041 --> 00:21:18.738
They didn’t do that back then.

463
00:21:18.738 --> 00:21:21.000
Told you he was going to sleep.

464
00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.500
<v ARSENEAULT>He likes the sound of your voice.

465
00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:23.857
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Agrees].

466
00:21:23.857 --> 00:21:26.357
So they . . . That’s how they made it.

467
00:21:26.357 --> 00:21:28.143
They made it by surviving

468
00:21:28.143 --> 00:21:29.214
<c.p>and they did.

469
00:21:29.214 --> 00:21:32.429
My momma sewed a lot, and she taught us.

470
00:21:32.429 --> 00:21:38.857
She sewed a lot so every . . . we didn’t go every once in a blue moon to a store.

471
00:21:38.857 --> 00:21:41.714
Other than that she’s on the sewing machine.

472
00:21:41.714 --> 00:21:43.500
We made everything we had.

473
00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:44.214
We made . . .

474
00:21:44.214 --> 00:21:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your mom . . .?

475
00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:47.684
<v HUNTSBERRY>My mother was Mary.

476
00:21:47.684 --> 00:21:48.526
Mary Louise.

477
00:21:48.526 --> 00:21:49.368
<v ARSENEAULT>Mary Louise.

478
00:21:49.368 --> 00:21:51.474
And when was she born?

479
00:21:51.474 --> 00:21:53.158
<v HUNTSBERRY>Momma was born 1933.

480
00:21:53.158 --> 00:21:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

481
00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:55.224
<v HUNTSBERRY>She was born 1933.

482
00:21:55.224 --> 00:21:56.142
June of 1933.

483
00:21:56.142 --> 00:22:01.038
She was born to [Olazine?] and Josephine, that’s . . . [Olazine?] and Josephine in the color picture there.

484
00:22:01.038 --> 00:22:04.710
So they was . . . like I said they was in the Westlake area.

485
00:22:04.710 --> 00:22:07.158
And that’s what I said where they moved.

486
00:22:07.158 --> 00:22:09.607
<v ARSENEAULT>Did her mom teach her how to sew?

487
00:22:09.607 --> 00:22:10.831
<v HUNTSBERRY>I never asked her.

488
00:22:10.831 --> 00:22:16.645
I never asked her because she taught us, and I never . . . She taught us how to crochet and everything.

489
00:22:16.645 --> 00:22:23.071
I never asked her because like I say, when I came up they was already deceased so I never asked her.

490
00:22:23.071 --> 00:22:25.825
Then when I started doing the family tree she

491
00:22:25.825 --> 00:22:33.169
<c.p>would laugh and she said, “Why is you digging up them dead folks?” and I’d be laughing I said, “Because I want to know.”

492
00:22:33.169 --> 00:22:34.087
I really did.

493
00:22:34.087 --> 00:22:38.678
I woke up one morning and said I had a dream about my Grandma Pinky.

494
00:22:38.678 --> 00:22:42.350
And I said I’m going to find out where she come from.

495
00:22:42.350 --> 00:22:44.186
And that’s when it started me.

496
00:22:44.186 --> 00:22:50.000
Then I had to do both sides, which my mother’s side, I’m up to the 1700s on them.

497
00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:52.054
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you know about them?

498
00:22:52.054 --> 00:22:52.739
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord.

499
00:22:52.739 --> 00:22:53.766
That’s the [Achans?].

500
00:22:53.766 --> 00:22:54.108
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

501
00:22:54.108 --> 00:22:58.216
<v HUNTSBERRY>How they came here . . . They was all in the Sunset area them,

502
00:22:58.216 --> 00:23:04.378
and then my great great grandmother, and that’s what I’m going to research, she was a Cherokee Indian.

503
00:23:04.378 --> 00:23:10.883
So that’s why I’m fixing to go into the Cherokee tribe and see if I can find her, so.

504
00:23:10.883 --> 00:23:14.649
But they was all in . . . That’s on that plaque up there.

505
00:23:14.649 --> 00:23:18.414
So they came from . . . they was all from down east, them.

506
00:23:18.414 --> 00:23:23.207
They all came . . . that I met . . . and Abbeville and all back up in there.

507
00:23:23.207 --> 00:23:26.288
So I said I’m going to finish researching that.

508
00:23:26.288 --> 00:23:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She was full Cherokee?

509
00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:28.349
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yes.

510
00:23:28.349 --> 00:23:29.047
Pure Cherokee.

511
00:23:29.047 --> 00:23:32.885
So we laughed about it because . . . we had a family reunion.

512
00:23:32.885 --> 00:23:40.910
We had a booklet with all the old pictures in it from my mom’s side so we did that. [Children in background] Dana,

513
00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:41.608
Dana . . . shhh.

514
00:23:41.608 --> 00:23:43.353
So we did her side.

515
00:23:43.353 --> 00:23:44.748
So and that’s why

516
00:23:44.748 --> 00:23:47.888
<c.p>Daddy said, “When you going to finish my side?”

517
00:23:47.888 --> 00:23:50.331
I said I’m going to finish it.

518
00:23:50.331 --> 00:23:59.752
But you see, I’m stuck with Grandma’s Pinky husband Ethan dad was Pete Mayo and he came from Africa on a Mayflower ship here in Lake Charles.

519
00:23:59.752 --> 00:24:04.986
A dock where the old used to be . . . Boat and [Forward?] used to be at.

520
00:24:04.986 --> 00:24:11.266
They used to have . . . The river used to run that way and that’s where the ship came in,

521
00:24:11.266 --> 00:24:14.406
and that’s where they sold them at as slaves.

522
00:24:14.406 --> 00:24:16.849
And so I cannot find him either.

523
00:24:16.849 --> 00:24:25.223
And I said I’m going to look because when you go into the plantation, they name them boy, girl, boy, girl, boy, girl, so.

524
00:24:25.223 --> 00:24:30.457
But on that Mayflower ship coming out of Africa, we found them in [Pete Mayo?].

525
00:24:30.457 --> 00:24:31.504
That was it.

526
00:24:31.504 --> 00:24:34.993
And that was in the early part of the . . . 1700s.

527
00:24:34.993 --> 00:24:35.691
Yeah, 1700s.

528
00:24:35.691 --> 00:24:40.924
So because when I was in the library yesterday the lady said, “Where you been?”

529
00:24:40.924 --> 00:24:46.158
And I had to laugh because I said, “Honey I had to take a break.”

530
00:24:46.158 --> 00:24:50.345
Because you have to take . . . You have to let your mind rest.

531
00:24:50.345 --> 00:24:56.975
You have to let your mind rest for a [day?] especially if you’re the only one doing this stuff.

532
00:24:56.975 --> 00:24:59.766
So, you have to let your mind rest.

533
00:24:59.766 --> 00:25:02.209
But it’s some good research to do.

534
00:25:02.209 --> 00:25:03.255
It really is.

535
00:25:03.255 --> 00:25:05.000
It’s some good research.

536
00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:07.667
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ve see that name in some old documents.

537
00:25:07.667 --> 00:25:08.333
<v HUNTSBERRY>Which one?

538
00:25:08.333 --> 00:25:09.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Mayo.

539
00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:09.526
<v HUNTSBERRY>Mayo.

540
00:25:09.526 --> 00:25:17.421
And back then, it was spelled M-E-A-U . . . M-A-Y-E-U- X . . . M-E-Y-U . . . It was something like that.

541
00:25:17.421 --> 00:25:19.526
It was spelled different.

542
00:25:19.526 --> 00:25:21.105
It wasn’t M-A-Y-O.

543
00:25:21.105 --> 00:25:24.263
It was spelled different back then.

544
00:25:24.263 --> 00:25:32.158
So that’s why I said you have to go back and look at all the

545
00:25:32.158 --> 00:25:36.895
<c.p>different spellings they had of it, which is good.

546
00:25:36.895 --> 00:25:38.474
Which is good.

547
00:25:38.474 --> 00:25:41.105
Which is very good, so.

548
00:25:41.105 --> 00:25:43.211
And then you have . . .

549
00:25:43.211 --> 00:25:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it like a French spelling before it became M-A-Y-O?

550
00:25:49.000 --> 00:26:00.723
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, well I think she told me she found another spelling yesterday and I told her I’m going to come back because . . . And do some more research on it.

551
00:26:00.723 --> 00:26:01.936
It probably was.

552
00:26:01.936 --> 00:26:03.149
It probably was.

553
00:26:03.149 --> 00:26:04.362
It probably was.

554
00:26:04.362 --> 00:26:06.383
Hold on [break in tape].

555
00:26:06.383 --> 00:26:07.191
The research.

556
00:26:07.191 --> 00:26:07.596
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

557
00:26:07.596 --> 00:26:11.234
<v HUNTSBERRY>I am going to finish the research of that.

558
00:26:11.234 --> 00:26:16.085
But like I said, it was a . . . It was ten of us.

559
00:26:16.085 --> 00:26:21.340
It was ten of us my mom and dad had, and really eleven.

560
00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:24.979
My oldest brother . . . well my oldest brother and . . . well,

561
00:26:24.979 --> 00:26:32.660
my three oldest brother . . . Well the first son my mother had, he died of . . . they call it crib death.

562
00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:37.106
I think he was only a few days old or something.

563
00:26:37.106 --> 00:26:41.957
And he died so my momma had . . . When she met my daddy,

564
00:26:41.957 --> 00:26:47.213
she had my two brothers, Jerry and . . . I mean Alvin and Jerry . . . Noel.

565
00:26:47.213 --> 00:26:51.660
And she married my daddy and it was ten of us.

566
00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:55.298
And to be honest, we didn’t want for nothing.

567
00:26:55.298 --> 00:26:59.340
You look back now you say, “Ooh y’all was poor.”

568
00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:00.553
No we wasn’t.

569
00:27:00.553 --> 00:27:01.766
We was happy.

570
00:27:01.766 --> 00:27:02.979
We was happy.

571
00:27:02.979 --> 00:27:06.213
We had chores, we really didn’t want anything.

572
00:27:06.213 --> 00:27:09.851
We didn’t really want . . . we wasn’t want for anything.

573
00:27:09.851 --> 00:27:11.468
We went to school.

574
00:27:11.468 --> 00:27:13.085
Everybody went to school.

575
00:27:13.085 --> 00:27:20.362
Like I said Mossville . . . when I entered Mossville, Mossville was from the first grade to the twelfth grade.

576
00:27:20.362 --> 00:27:28.447
Then back in the early part of the seventies, that’s when they . . . decided to cut it to a junior school.

577
00:27:28.447 --> 00:27:32.489
And that’s when we went off to Sulphur High, us.

578
00:27:32.489 --> 00:27:35.319
And the other people went to Westlake

579
00:27:35.319 --> 00:27:35.723
<c.p>High.

580
00:27:35.723 --> 00:27:37.340
But we had fun.

581
00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:43.000
Now my oldest brother, he graduated from Mossville in ’69, so he graduated . . .

582
00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:44.250
<v ARSENEAULT>He was the last class?

583
00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:45.750
Or one of the last class . . .?

584
00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:47.250
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah he was the last one.

585
00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:48.250
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s your brother’s name?

586
00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:49.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Alvin Noel.

587
00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:49.444
<v ARSENEAULT>Alvin Noel.

588
00:27:49.444 --> 00:27:49.667
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Agrees].

589
00:27:49.667 --> 00:27:50.556
Yeah, he graduated from . . .

590
00:27:50.556 --> 00:27:52.111
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know all your sibling’s names?

591
00:27:52.111 --> 00:27:53.000
Can you remember?

592
00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:54.400
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord yes.

593
00:27:54.400 --> 00:27:55.333
Oh, yes.

594
00:27:55.333 --> 00:28:01.400
So it’s Alvin Noel, Jerry Noel, then it’s Josephine, and then it’s me,

595
00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:07.933
I’m Jawanna, then we got Henry, Coleman, and Christopher, Paul, and Latonya, oh yeah [?].

596
00:28:07.933 --> 00:28:08.867
So . . . Oh,

597
00:28:08.867 --> 00:28:18.200
yeah. [Tarsen?] . . . and only really . . . we just only have . . . In our family right now we only have two is deceased,

598
00:28:18.200 --> 00:28:22.867
which is [Tarsen?] and my mom is deceased right now.

599
00:28:22.867 --> 00:28:30.333
So they the only two in the family, in our immediate family right now, is deceased.

600
00:28:30.333 --> 00:28:31.267
Oh, yeah.

601
00:28:31.267 --> 00:28:32.200
So we . . .

602
00:28:32.200 --> 00:28:36.867
<v ARSENEAULT>So growing up, what were the geographic boundaries of Mossville?

603
00:28:36.867 --> 00:28:40.133
Like how did you know where to . . .?

604
00:28:40.133 --> 00:28:42.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Where to go?

605
00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:42.336
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

606
00:28:42.336 --> 00:28:48.381
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, it was easy we just . . . Matter of fact, we stayed on the street and played with everybody,

607
00:28:48.381 --> 00:28:54.090
but Old Spanish Trail, we’ll go down to by the school and we’ll go to Bel Air.

608
00:28:54.090 --> 00:28:55.769
So it was real easy.

609
00:28:55.769 --> 00:29:02.821
You could either walk . . . we did a lot of walking and a few ride . . . We rode bikes but it’s not often,

610
00:29:02.821 --> 00:29:04.164
but we did walking.

611
00:29:04.164 --> 00:29:07.187
Wherever we wanted to go we walked, as kids.

612
00:29:07.187 --> 00:29:11.216
And we’ll walk . . . Like on Saturdays and Sunday, me and my cousins,

613
00:29:11.216 --> 00:29:17.933
we’ll walk past Mossville School and go back there to Bel Air to buy frozen cups and stuff like that.

614
00:29:17.933 --> 00:29:20.955
We’ll . . . [Speaking to child?] Yeah, you turn buttons off.

615
00:29:20.955 --> 00:29:21.963
We’ll go walk . . .

616
00:29:21.963 --> 00:29:22.634
<v TANGLAO>It’s okay.

617
00:29:22.634 --> 00:29:23.642
<v HUNTSBERRY>It was okay?

618
00:29:23.642 --> 00:29:25.321
He didn’t turn it off?

619
00:29:25.321 --> 00:29:27.000
Girl get your cup.

620
00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:28.014
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ll watch him.

621
00:29:28.014 --> 00:29:29.028
I’ll guard it.

622
00:29:29.028 --> 00:29:33.761
<v HUNTSBERRY>So most of the time, like I say we did a lot of walking.

623
00:29:33.761 --> 00:29:36.127
We did a lot of walking, so.

624
00:29:36.127 --> 00:29:39.169
It was easy and we had a lot of . . .

625
00:29:39.169 --> 00:29:40.521
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were your neighbors?

626
00:29:40.521 --> 00:29:47.282
<v HUNTSBERRY>The neighbors was Peters . . . We had the Peters next to us, the Montgomery’s, and we had the . . . What is was?

627
00:29:47.282 --> 00:29:47.620
Rougeau.

628
00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:48.296
Mr. Rougeau.

629
00:29:48.296 --> 00:29:49.648
Lord bless his heart.

630
00:29:49.648 --> 00:29:51.000
He’s another one.

631
00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:52.758
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he a white man?

632
00:29:52.758 --> 00:29:54.516
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, he was a black . . .

633
00:29:54.516 --> 00:29:56.275
<v ARSENEAULT>He was a black man?

634
00:29:56.275 --> 00:29:57.330
<v HUNTSBERRY>He was black.

635
00:29:57.330 --> 00:29:59.088
He stayed next to us.

636
00:29:59.088 --> 00:30:00.846
He had a garden, too.

637
00:30:00.846 --> 00:30:01.549
Mr. Rougeau.

638
00:30:01.549 --> 00:30:05.066
And then we had Pete Moss, the Praters, the Gasaways.

639
00:30:05.066 --> 00:30:05.769
Oh, yeah.

640
00:30:05.769 --> 00:30:06.824
I could remember.

641
00:30:06.824 --> 00:30:07.527
The Montgomery’s.

642
00:30:07.527 --> 00:30:08.231
Oh, yeah.

643
00:30:08.231 --> 00:30:09.989
It was a big thing.

644
00:30:09.989 --> 00:30:12.451
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about Pete Moss?

645
00:30:12.451 --> 00:30:12.802
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh!

646
00:30:12.802 --> 00:30:17.725
Mr. Pete Moss riding down that road with that horse and that buggy, honey.

647
00:30:17.725 --> 00:30:18.429
Oh yeah.

648
00:30:18.429 --> 00:30:21.945
Everywhere he go he had his horse and his buggy.

649
00:30:21.945 --> 00:30:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s funny.

650
00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:23.359
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yes.

651
00:30:23.359 --> 00:30:24.793
And we had cars.

652
00:30:24.793 --> 00:30:26.946
We had cars at the time.

653
00:30:26.946 --> 00:30:28.739
<v ARSENEAULT>But he was still using . . .?

654
00:30:28.739 --> 00:30:31.250
<v HUNTSBERRY>He still rode his horse and buggy.

655
00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:37.348
He’ll tag along going down there to Market Basket or Piggly Wiggly with that horse and buggy.

656
00:30:37.348 --> 00:30:38.424
He will go.

657
00:30:38.424 --> 00:30:39.500
He will go.

658
00:30:39.500 --> 00:30:42.370
And I say, “Momma look at Pete Moss.”

659
00:30:42.370 --> 00:30:44.522
And he . . . That’s what he did.

660
00:30:44.522 --> 00:30:46.315
He didn’t have a car.

661
00:30:46.315 --> 00:30:48.826
He just had his horse and buggy.

662
00:30:48.826 --> 00:30:52.413
Yeah he would drive his . . . ride that horse and buggy.

663
00:30:52.413 --> 00:30:56.000
Him and his wife Virginia . . . Virginia was her name.

664
00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:59.586
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that family who Mossville was named after?

665
00:30:59.586 --> 00:31:02.276
<v HUNTSBERRY>No [disagrees] it was Jack Moss.

666
00:31:02.276 --> 00:31:07.207
Mr. Jack Moss dad, Jack Moss was our janitor at Mossville.

667
00:31:07.207 --> 00:31:18.862
That was his dad that originally found . . . They did the history so it said Jack Moss was the one that . . . first to settle there or something.

668
00:31:18.862 --> 00:31:21.103
No, no not Mr. Moss.

669
00:31:21.103 --> 00:31:24.241
He was between him and Mr. Young.

670
00:31:24.241 --> 00:31:26.034
Them horse and buggies.

671
00:31:26.034 --> 00:31:31.414
Oh yeah, and that was in the early part of the 70s.

672
00:31:31.414 --> 00:31:39.931
They was still . . . you could say . . . late 60s to early 70s they was still riding their horse and buggies.

673
00:31:39.931 --> 00:31:43.069
Still had them horse and buggies, honey.

674
00:31:43.069 --> 00:31:44.862
And he didn’t have . . .

675
00:31:44.862 --> 00:31:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That must have been a sight.

676
00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:51.134
<v HUNTSBERRY>He didn’t have electricity in his house.

677
00:31:51.134 --> 00:31:54.269
He’ll light candles or all that stuff.

678
00:31:54.269 --> 00:31:57.403
He just stayed for the old school.

679
00:31:57.403 --> 00:31:58.746
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he garden?

680
00:31:58.746 --> 00:31:59.194
<v HUNTSBERRY>Pete?

681
00:31:59.194 --> 00:32:00.537
Let me see.

682
00:32:00.537 --> 00:32:07.254
I think he did, yes he did because everybody back then did on that street.

683
00:32:07.254 --> 00:32:10.836
Everybody had gardens and raised animals back then.

684
00:32:10.836 --> 00:32:11.284
Yeah.

685
00:32:11.284 --> 00:32:13.075
Yeah we had fun.

686
00:32:13.075 --> 00:32:14.418
We had fun.

687
00:32:14.418 --> 00:32:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did Mossville have any other nicknames or . . .?

688
00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:25.231
<v HUNTSBERRY>Not as I knowed of, no. I wouldn’t . . . My brother would because I was really the quiet one.

689
00:32:25.231 --> 00:32:29.650
My brother and them was ones that got out there [laughs].

690
00:32:29.650 --> 00:32:31.658
No, I don’t think so.

691
00:32:31.658 --> 00:32:33.265
I don’t think so.

692
00:32:33.265 --> 00:32:35.274
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the different neighborhoods?

693
00:32:35.274 --> 00:32:38.889
Was the Prince Street area, was that a neighborhood?

694
00:32:38.889 --> 00:32:40.496
Or did y’all just . . .?

695
00:32:40.496 --> 00:32:42.504
<v HUNTSBERRY>That was the Queenboro neighborhood.

696
00:32:42.504 --> 00:32:42.906
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

697
00:32:42.906 --> 00:32:45.316
<v HUNTSBERRY>And, but it wasn’t no different.

698
00:32:45.316 --> 00:32:52.949
We all got together, we all . . . All the children played together and we didn’t go that far into Queensboro,

699
00:32:52.949 --> 00:32:56.564
we call it Queensboro, we didn’t go that far.

700
00:32:56.564 --> 00:33:05.000
We would go further than up the street to my auntie [children crying] you can pause it [break in tape].

701
00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:07.955
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright so who were the community leaders growing up?

702
00:33:07.955 --> 00:33:09.925
Did y’all have community leaders or . . .?

703
00:33:09.925 --> 00:33:11.567
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Disagrees] no. No, we didn’t.

704
00:33:11.567 --> 00:33:13.537
No we didn’t have no leaders.

705
00:33:13.537 --> 00:33:17.806
Like I said we just all just played together and stuff like that,

706
00:33:17.806 --> 00:33:23.388
but nobody was . . . we didn’t have . . . it was strange but like I was telling the kids now,

707
00:33:23.388 --> 00:33:26.343
we didn’t have like bullets and stuff like that.

708
00:33:26.343 --> 00:33:28.313
We didn’t have all of that.

709
00:33:28.313 --> 00:33:30.284
We didn’t have all of that.

710
00:33:30.284 --> 00:33:33.896
Everybody played and got along and we went our separate ways.

711
00:33:33.896 --> 00:33:35.866
But the majority of the time,

712
00:33:35.866 --> 00:33:41.776
you really maybe . . . once every blue moon you might get together and play ball or something like that.

713
00:33:41.776 --> 00:33:45.060
But majority of the time we all had household chores.

714
00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:47.030
We had to do our chores.

715
00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Like what kind of stuff?

716
00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:54.010
<v HUNTSBERRY>We had to clean, feed the horses, cows, chickens, we had to do all of that.

717
00:33:54.010 --> 00:33:55.889
Cut yards and stuff like that.

718
00:33:55.889 --> 00:33:57.141
Wash clothes and stuff.

719
00:33:57.141 --> 00:33:58.707
So we stayed constantly busy.

720
00:33:58.707 --> 00:33:59.020
Everybody.

721
00:33:59.020 --> 00:34:01.838
So . . . And then when we did have a break,

722
00:34:01.838 --> 00:34:07.475
then that’s when we’ll get together, all the kids in the neighborhood, we’ll play softball, baseball, or something.

723
00:34:07.475 --> 00:34:08.414
We’ll do that.

724
00:34:08.414 --> 00:34:11.545
But other than that we basically was home doing chores.

725
00:34:11.545 --> 00:34:13.111
We had chores to do.

726
00:34:13.111 --> 00:34:16.869
And like I tell them, these kids today is just . . . it’s unreal.

727
00:34:16.869 --> 00:34:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How’d you know what you’re supposed to be doing?

728
00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:21.767
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, we . . . honey Momma tell you.

729
00:34:21.767 --> 00:34:24.123
You got a week to do this, a

730
00:34:24.123 --> 00:34:30.308
<c.p>week . . . Like I had a week to wash dishes, and then next week my sister washed dishes, and brothers and them.

731
00:34:30.308 --> 00:34:32.959
So you had . . . You knew when your week came.

732
00:34:32.959 --> 00:34:36.788
And trust me if you didn’t wash those dishes you had it coming.

733
00:34:36.788 --> 00:34:43.562
And then you had the . . . I don’t know if we had . . . she didn’t list us how to go out and feed the animals.

734
00:34:43.562 --> 00:34:46.507
We just had to go out there and do it.

735
00:34:46.507 --> 00:34:53.281
There’s so many animals we just had to . . . everybody just went out there and fed animals and cut grass, and we worked gardens.

736
00:34:53.281 --> 00:34:59.760
And then we’ll come home after school, me and my sister, and get on the floor with Momma and we’ll do quilts.

737
00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:01.527
So we constantly was doing things . . .

738
00:35:01.527 --> 00:35:03.000
<v CHILD>Maw maw I’m thirsty.

739
00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:04.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Go in the room.

740
00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:05.500
<v CHILD>Can I get something to drink?

741
00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:08.500
<v HUNTSBERRY>I’m going to give you something to drink in a little bit.

742
00:35:08.500 --> 00:35:09.500
Go in the room.

743
00:35:09.500 --> 00:35:11.000
<v CHILD>Can I have some milk?

744
00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:13.023
<v HUNTSBERRY>We constantly had chores to do.

745
00:35:13.023 --> 00:35:14.709
And that’s how it was.

746
00:35:14.709 --> 00:35:16.395
That’s how we came up.

747
00:35:16.395 --> 00:35:18.419
So, there wasn’t no movie time.

748
00:35:18.419 --> 00:35:25.500
Every blue moon my daddy might take us to the movies or something like that, but we didn’t have all that.

749
00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:27.186
We didn’t have all that.

750
00:35:27.186 --> 00:35:32.581
You either watched T.V. . . . and the only time we watched T.V. we only had three stations.

751
00:35:32.581 --> 00:35:38.988
Back then you only had three stations to watch, so basically home . . . We was basically home, so that’s us.

752
00:35:38.988 --> 00:35:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who gardened?

753
00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:40.800
<v HUNTSBERRY>My mom.

754
00:35:40.800 --> 00:35:44.000
My mom did a lot, and we did.

755
00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:48.000
My momma did and we’d be right there with her.

756
00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:54.000
We’d go out and then . . . Go out in the woods and pick blueberries, blackberries, whatever.

757
00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:56.400
So we did all of that.

758
00:35:56.400 --> 00:35:58.400
We did all of that.

759
00:35:58.400 --> 00:36:00.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she plant by the Almanac?

760
00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:01.200
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yes.

761
00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:02.400
Yes, she did.

762
00:36:02.400 --> 00:36:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Or by the moon?

763
00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:05.600
<v HUNTSBERRY>She believed in that.

764
00:36:05.600 --> 00:36:07.600
She believed in all that.

765
00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:08.400
Oh, yes.

766
00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:10.000
She believed in that.

767
00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:11.600
She believed in that.

768
00:36:11.600 --> 00:36:14.400
Yeah, we raised a lot of stuff.

769
00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of . . .?

770
00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:17.343
<v HUNTSBERRY>Vegetables and stuff?

771
00:36:17.343 --> 00:36:27.190
Oh we had them all, snap beans, purple hurled beans, we had corn, we did . . . she did okra, tomatoes, we had potatoes.

772
00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:28.981
We even did potatoes.

773
00:36:28.981 --> 00:36:31.667
What else she used to do?

774
00:36:31.667 --> 00:36:33.010
Some hot peppers.

775
00:36:33.010 --> 00:36:35.695
What else . . . she had mustard greens.

776
00:36:35.695 --> 00:36:37.933
Oh, Lord she did it.

777
00:36:37.933 --> 00:36:39.276
We had fun.

778
00:36:39.276 --> 00:36:40.619
We had fun.

779
00:36:40.619 --> 00:36:49.124
We had to pick all of that, clean it, and can it, and do what we had to do.

780
00:36:49.124 --> 00:36:52.705
We had to do all of that, so.

781
00:36:52.705 --> 00:36:54.495
<v ARSENEAULT>So what is canning?

782
00:36:54.495 --> 00:36:59.867
Because I know that’s not something anyone really does too much anymore.

783
00:36:59.867 --> 00:37:01.210
<v HUNTSBERRY>I still do.

784
00:37:01.210 --> 00:37:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You still can?

785
00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:03.909
<v HUNTSBERRY>I still can.

786
00:37:03.909 --> 00:37:04.212
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

787
00:37:04.212 --> 00:37:09.364
<v HUNTSBERRY>Well I canned some figs the other day and when sister and pastor asked me he said,

788
00:37:09.364 --> 00:37:14.212
“You going to can me some figs?” and I fell out laughing I said, “Oh, yeah.”

789
00:37:14.212 --> 00:37:14.818
We can.

790
00:37:14.818 --> 00:37:22.394
It depends on what . . . Like figs and stuff you do it with a lot of sugar and water and it cooks down into a syrup.

791
00:37:22.394 --> 00:37:25.121
And you produce cook down and we do that.

792
00:37:25.121 --> 00:37:27.848
And then she did a lot of . . . The tomatoes,

793
00:37:27.848 --> 00:37:34.212
she would boil them and they would put them in vinegar and stuff like that and put them in a jar.

794
00:37:34.212 --> 00:37:39.364
And they’ll set and then when you need them you just open the jar and use them.

795
00:37:39.364 --> 00:37:43.909
Okra, you’ll pre-cook your okra and put it in a jar and let it sit.

796
00:37:43.909 --> 00:37:45.424
We did all of that.

797
00:37:45.424 --> 00:37:46.636
We did all that.

798
00:37:46.636 --> 00:37:50.576
Matter of fact, I got some canned figs in my box right now.

799
00:37:50.576 --> 00:37:51.182
Oh, yeah.

800
00:37:51.182 --> 00:37:53.000
I love my figs honey.

801
00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:54.857
<v ARSENEAULT>Put it on some corn bread?

802
00:37:54.857 --> 00:37:56.405
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Disagrees] girl, some hot biscuits.

803
00:37:56.405 --> 00:37:57.952
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, that does sound good.

804
00:37:57.952 --> 00:37:58.881
<v HUNTSBERRY>Some hot biscuits.

805
00:37:58.881 --> 00:37:59.500
Oh, yeah.

806
00:37:59.500 --> 00:38:01.357
So we did all of that.

807
00:38:01.357 --> 00:38:02.286
It was good.

808
00:38:02.286 --> 00:38:04.143
That was some good eating baby.

809
00:38:04.143 --> 00:38:06.000
That was some good eating.

810
00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:07.146
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the animals?

811
00:38:07.146 --> 00:38:07.718
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord.

812
00:38:07.718 --> 00:38:11.155
Them dog gone chickens look like . . . I tell my momma look like . . .

813
00:38:11.155 --> 00:38:12.873
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of chickens were they?

814
00:38:12.873 --> 00:38:13.732
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, my God.

815
00:38:13.732 --> 00:38:20.606
Momma would have all these different chickens and she would set out in these magazines and order these chickens that come on the truck.

816
00:38:20.606 --> 00:38:21.178
Oh, Lord.

817
00:38:21.178 --> 00:38:25.188
These chickens that lay these different color eggs and the fur on their legs.

818
00:38:25.188 --> 00:38:26.906
Oh, my momma had them all.

819
00:38:26.906 --> 00:38:28.052
She had them all.

820
00:38:28.052 --> 00:38:32.920
And I used to get so mad I’d say, “Momma when you going to stop ordering chickens?”

821
00:38:32.920 --> 00:38:35.211
She’d say, “Girl you need to eat, huh?”

822
00:38:35.211 --> 00:38:37.216
And that’s how she would say it.

823
00:38:37.216 --> 00:38:43.803
And we had some chickens, some gooses, some . . . I mean we even had different geeses that laid colorful eggs and stuff like this.

824
00:38:43.803 --> 00:38:48.099
She would get on there and she’ll find all these things and my daddy said,

825
00:38:48.099 --> 00:38:52.681
“There she go again,” and “you got to go pick them up at the bus station.”

826
00:38:52.681 --> 00:38:56.117
Because they’ll come on the bus; you have to go get them.

827
00:38:56.117 --> 00:38:57.836
Yeah so she had some chickens.

828
00:38:57.836 --> 00:38:58.122
Wild

829
00:38:58.122 --> 00:39:01.272
<c.p>chickens, and guineas . . . Them darn guineas, you never can catch them.

830
00:39:01.272 --> 00:39:04.423
My sister was the only one who could catch them guineas.

831
00:39:04.423 --> 00:39:07.000
I ain’t never seen nobody catch a guinea.

832
00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:08.429
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they lay eggs, too?

833
00:39:08.429 --> 00:39:08.714
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Agrees].

834
00:39:08.714 --> 00:39:10.143
But they were so fast.

835
00:39:10.143 --> 00:39:11.286
They were so fast.

836
00:39:11.286 --> 00:39:15.571
And she the only one could catch a guinea, and we just laugh at her.

837
00:39:15.571 --> 00:39:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She was fast, too?

838
00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:18.043
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, she wasn’t fast.

839
00:39:18.043 --> 00:39:20.391
She’d lay on that ground and crawl to them.

840
00:39:20.391 --> 00:39:23.522
She would crawl until she catch them and we would just laugh.

841
00:39:23.522 --> 00:39:27.174
I say, “I’m not getting on no ground to crawl and get no guinea.”

842
00:39:27.174 --> 00:39:28.478
And she would catch them.

843
00:39:28.478 --> 00:39:29.522
She would catch them.

844
00:39:29.522 --> 00:39:30.565
She would catch them.

845
00:39:30.565 --> 00:39:31.609
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you eat guinea?

846
00:39:31.609 --> 00:39:32.913
<v HUNTSBERRY>No I never ate it.

847
00:39:32.913 --> 00:39:33.957
I never ate it.

848
00:39:33.957 --> 00:39:34.739
Momma cook it.

849
00:39:34.739 --> 00:39:36.043
Now, it’s the funniest thing.

850
00:39:36.043 --> 00:39:40.478
Like the chicken, I would eat it if I don’t see you put it in a pot.

851
00:39:40.478 --> 00:39:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

852
00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:45.725
<v HUNTSBERRY>If I see you put it . . . Kill it and put it in a pot, I’m not going to

853
00:39:45.725 --> 00:39:46.250
<c.p>eat it.

854
00:39:46.250 --> 00:39:47.562
It was the amazing thing.

855
00:39:47.562 --> 00:39:54.912
So Momma had to make sure she cook it . . . she had to make sure she cook it before I get off of school for me to eat it.

856
00:39:54.912 --> 00:39:57.012
Because other than that, I wouldn’t eat it.

857
00:39:57.012 --> 00:39:57.538
I wouldn’t.

858
00:39:57.538 --> 00:39:58.588
I say, “Oh, Momma.”

859
00:39:58.588 --> 00:39:59.900
I play with them things.

860
00:39:59.900 --> 00:40:00.950
I couldn’t eat that.

861
00:40:00.950 --> 00:40:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So a pet?

862
00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:04.154
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah and that’s what I told her.

863
00:40:04.154 --> 00:40:06.615
She said, “Oh, girl you need to eat.”

864
00:40:06.615 --> 00:40:09.692
She said you need to eat and we just laughed.

865
00:40:09.692 --> 00:40:10.923
But that and the . . .

866
00:40:10.923 --> 00:40:12.154
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she kill them?

867
00:40:12.154 --> 00:40:12.462
<v HUNTSBERRY>Huh?

868
00:40:12.462 --> 00:40:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she kill it?

869
00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:14.648
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, yeah.

870
00:40:14.648 --> 00:40:15.296
<v ARSENEAULT>She did?

871
00:40:15.296 --> 00:40:19.507
<v HUNTSBERRY>She rings they neck and cut them off and she would clean them.

872
00:40:19.507 --> 00:40:28.254
She would . . . we would take and . . . they had wax and you put it in hot water and you dip the chicken in to take the feathers off.

873
00:40:28.254 --> 00:40:30.845
And it took the feathers clean off it.

874
00:40:30.845 --> 00:40:32.789
And that’s how we did that.

875
00:40:32.789 --> 00:40:34.085
And so . . . Oh yeah.

876
00:40:34.085 --> 00:40:34.732
Oh, yes.

877
00:40:34.732 --> 00:40:38.620
So like I said we lived on . . . we practically lived on the . . .

878
00:40:38.620 --> 00:40:39.268
<c.p>Our land.

879
00:40:39.268 --> 00:40:42.183
And so it . . . Like I say it was good.

880
00:40:42.183 --> 00:40:45.423
And then what pigs . . . she didn’t have . . . we had pigs,

881
00:40:45.423 --> 00:40:52.873
but she killed her pigs and then next one up, Mr. Peters would kill his, and they’d just swap meat back and forth.

882
00:40:52.873 --> 00:40:54.817
And that’s how we made it.

883
00:40:54.817 --> 00:40:58.380
That’s how we made it. [Whispering to child] stop it, stop.

884
00:40:58.380 --> 00:41:00.000
You can’t have that.

885
00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:04.766
<v ARSENEAULT>He wants to touch everything he can’t touch.

886
00:41:04.766 --> 00:41:06.553
<v HUNTSBERRY>[To child] Yeah.

887
00:41:06.553 --> 00:41:08.936
You can’t have that.

888
00:41:08.936 --> 00:41:12.511
So that’s how we made it.

889
00:41:12.511 --> 00:41:14.894
<v ARSENEAULT>So no community leaders.

890
00:41:14.894 --> 00:41:17.872
So what about Mr. Rigmaiden?

891
00:41:17.872 --> 00:41:19.064
Remember him?

892
00:41:19.064 --> 00:41:20.851
Anything about him?

893
00:41:20.851 --> 00:41:23.234
<v HUNTSBERRY>Mr. Rigmaiden . . . Carl Rigmaiden?

894
00:41:23.234 --> 00:41:25.617
Which . . . It was Carl?

895
00:41:25.617 --> 00:41:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I think so.

896
00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:34.038
<v HUNTSBERRY>I didn’t . . . I never remember Mr. Carl, but Mr. Carl had passed a long time ago when we were small.

897
00:41:34.038 --> 00:41:47.623
His wife, Mrs. . . . I never remember . . . Like I said the elderly people we didn’t hang around because the children did not go . . . if they came and talked to your mom . . . if they came and talked to mom and them we was in the back room.

898
00:41:47.623 --> 00:41:50.943
We never did sit in the front room, listen to conversations.

899
00:41:50.943 --> 00:41:51.849
We never did.

900
00:41:51.849 --> 00:41:53.358
Or we would go outside.

901
00:41:53.358 --> 00:41:58.491
They would tell you to go outside because they didn’t want us to hear what they said.

902
00:41:58.491 --> 00:42:00.604
Or either they’ll start talking in French.

903
00:42:00.604 --> 00:42:04.528
If we passed through the room they’ll start talking in French because they

904
00:42:04.528 --> 00:42:12.075
<c.p>didn’t want us . . . and they wouldn’t teach us French because they didn’t want us to know what they was talking about at the particular time.

905
00:42:12.075 --> 00:42:19.925
So like I said the elderly ladies that come and they’ll mingle and talk or drink coffee, we never was around them because we wasn’t allowed.

906
00:42:19.925 --> 00:42:22.642
Because they’ll tell you quick go in the back.

907
00:42:22.642 --> 00:42:28.679
And then if you didn’t move, she’ll [point?] and when she [point?] you, you knew you had something coming later.

908
00:42:28.679 --> 00:42:30.189
And that’s how that was.

909
00:42:30.189 --> 00:42:32.000
That’s how that was, so.

910
00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:35.749
<v ARSENEAULT>So how were you expected to behave in front of adults?

911
00:42:35.749 --> 00:42:37.113
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, baby you knew.

912
00:42:37.113 --> 00:42:40.180
If you didn’t knew, you knew after they left.

913
00:42:40.180 --> 00:42:41.543
And they didn’t play.

914
00:42:41.543 --> 00:42:43.248
Them old people didn’t play.

915
00:42:43.248 --> 00:42:49.383
And we didn’t catch . . . Well not me, me and my other older brothers, we didn’t catch no whooping.

916
00:42:49.383 --> 00:42:52.791
But them other ones behind me, they caught some whoopings.

917
00:42:52.791 --> 00:42:55.518
You was . . . it’s just something you didn’t do.

918
00:42:55.518 --> 00:42:58.926
We didn’t . . . We just didn’t hang around the elderly people.

919
00:42:58.926 --> 00:43:06.424
You go and you bring stuff in the brown bag, and you’d better not open that brown bag and that was it.

920
00:43:06.424 --> 00:43:10.174
They’ll tell you quick, “Bring mister so-and-so . . .” and that was it.

921
00:43:10.174 --> 00:43:11.878
And that’s how it was.

922
00:43:11.878 --> 00:43:20.399
Or if you go to the neighbors and get something for my mom, they’ll fold that bag so neat they’ll say, “Now don’t you look.”

923
00:43:20.399 --> 00:43:22.103
“I’m not looking in it.”

924
00:43:22.103 --> 00:43:24.830
And you’d better not look in that bag.

925
00:43:24.830 --> 00:43:26.534
And that’s how they was.

926
00:43:26.534 --> 00:43:27.897
That’s how they was.

927
00:43:27.897 --> 00:43:31.987
And it was just food but, that’s how the elderly people was.

928
00:43:31.987 --> 00:43:38.804
And we was more . . . that’s what I told the kids, we was more respectable coming up than these kids today.

929
00:43:38.804 --> 00:43:40.508
Like these kids today [laughs].

930
00:43:40.508 --> 00:43:41.531
I don’t know.

931
00:43:41.531 --> 00:43:43.235
We didn’t have all that.

932
00:43:43.235 --> 00:43:44.939
We didn’t have all that.

933
00:43:44.939 --> 00:43:51.756
And like I say it was a . . . we all had . . . they all had big children all . . . The family was huge.

934
00:43:51.756 --> 00:43:53.801
And we didn’t have all that.

935
00:43:53.801 --> 00:43:56.868
But then Daddy, when he whoops my brother and

936
00:43:56.868 --> 00:43:59.254
<c.p>. . . he whoops you like once a year.

937
00:43:59.254 --> 00:44:02.662
And when he whoop you, he whoop you for everything.

938
00:44:02.662 --> 00:44:05.048
So they knew that was a whooping.

939
00:44:05.048 --> 00:44:13.228
But mom used to be the one that whoops all the time and stuff like that, but them brothers of mine, Lord have mercy.

940
00:44:13.228 --> 00:44:14.932
That’s all I can say.

941
00:44:14.932 --> 00:44:16.296
They was something else.

942
00:44:16.296 --> 00:44:18.000
They was something else.

943
00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:19.211
<v ARSENEAULT>They got into trouble?

944
00:44:19.211 --> 00:44:21.330
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord they always get in trouble.

945
00:44:21.330 --> 00:44:26.477
They always doing something they had no business . . . Half the time I wouldn’t even know because me,

946
00:44:26.477 --> 00:44:28.899
like I said, I stayed in the house.

947
00:44:28.899 --> 00:44:34.954
A lot of people did not know I was a part of the family because they say, “Where you been?”

948
00:44:34.954 --> 00:44:41.312
I was always in the house because I . . . Either I walked out to my auntie and visited my cousin and them,

949
00:44:41.312 --> 00:44:42.826
and then I come back.

950
00:44:42.826 --> 00:44:46.761
I never was the one to just run up and down the street.

951
00:44:46.761 --> 00:44:47.670
I never did.

952
00:44:47.670 --> 00:44:49.183
So it was . . . It’s amazing.

953
00:44:49.183 --> 00:44:51.000
And so it was good.

954
00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:52.286
<v ARSENEAULT>What about Mr. Montgomery?

955
00:44:52.286 --> 00:44:54.216
Was he like a community leader?

956
00:44:54.216 --> 00:44:54.859
Mr. . . . Valery?

957
00:44:54.859 --> 00:44:55.181
<v HUNTSBERRY>Valery?

958
00:44:55.181 --> 00:44:57.110
Mr. Valery owned the Paradise Club.

959
00:44:57.110 --> 00:45:02.577
He had the Paradise Club and my momma used to run the café at the Paradise Club.

960
00:45:02.577 --> 00:45:05.150
And honey, we made some money off Valery.

961
00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:06.115
I remember that.

962
00:45:06.115 --> 00:45:12.868
My daddy said . . . He told my momma, me, and my sister, he said, “They over there collecting five dollars a car.”

963
00:45:12.868 --> 00:45:14.797
They would park in our yard.

964
00:45:14.797 --> 00:45:16.727
We had a big big yard.

965
00:45:16.727 --> 00:45:22.837
And when they have the big dances and stuff at the club for Mr. Valery, he would put on,

966
00:45:22.837 --> 00:45:28.626
people couldn’t park at the club because it was full, so they had to park in the yards.

967
00:45:28.626 --> 00:45:29.590
So we start

968
00:45:29.590 --> 00:45:32.163
<c.p>charging, “Y’all want to park in our yard?

969
00:45:32.163 --> 00:45:33.771
We going to charge y’all.”

970
00:45:33.771 --> 00:45:36.987
So we charged five dollars to park in the yard.

971
00:45:36.987 --> 00:45:43.740
And we did and Daddy said, “Them girls over there charging,” and Momma never told us nothing so we charged them.

972
00:45:43.740 --> 00:45:45.670
That’s how we made our money.

973
00:45:45.670 --> 00:45:52.101
Charging the people the . . . And he said, “Now them people that got [?] back there,” I say, “I can’t help them.”

974
00:45:52.101 --> 00:45:55.317
That’s what I told him, “I can’t push no car.”

975
00:45:55.317 --> 00:45:56.282
It was funny.

976
00:45:56.282 --> 00:45:59.498
But we . . . he . . . like I say he worked that club.

977
00:45:59.498 --> 00:46:04.000
And then I think he worked . . . I think he worked at the plant.

978
00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:04.615
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Valery?

979
00:46:04.615 --> 00:46:04.923
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah.

980
00:46:04.923 --> 00:46:06.154
<v ARSENEAULT>I think he did.

981
00:46:06.154 --> 00:46:08.000
I think it was Conoco.

982
00:46:08.000 --> 00:46:08.304
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah.

983
00:46:08.304 --> 00:46:12.261
And him and Mrs. Eula . . . Mrs. Eula would cook in them big pots.

984
00:46:12.261 --> 00:46:18.652
They all had big pots back then, Momma, Mrs. Eula, Ms. Peters, and the . . . Ms. Agnes Montgomery, and my Auntie Josephine.

985
00:46:18.652 --> 00:46:22.913
They would cook in them big big pots because they families was so huge.

986
00:46:22.913 --> 00:46:26.261
And they’ll say, “Come in here baby and get you something.”

987
00:46:26.261 --> 00:46:27.174
“I’m not hungry.”

988
00:46:27.174 --> 00:46:28.696
“I didn’t asked you that.

989
00:46:28.696 --> 00:46:31.435
I said come in here and get you something.”

990
00:46:31.435 --> 00:46:32.957
And that’s how they did.

991
00:46:32.957 --> 00:46:35.696
That’s how they took care . . . [speaking to child] Stop.

992
00:46:35.696 --> 00:46:36.304
Stop baby.

993
00:46:36.304 --> 00:46:37.826
So that’s how they did.

994
00:46:37.826 --> 00:46:41.478
But like I said Valery . . . had that club for a good while.

995
00:46:41.478 --> 00:46:43.609
That was the biggest club in Mossville.

996
00:46:43.609 --> 00:46:45.130
That was the hottest club.

997
00:46:45.130 --> 00:46:48.478
Looked like . . . we used to say, “Where these peoples coming from?”

998
00:46:48.478 --> 00:46:54.565
And they would come on Friday nights to do dances and have . . . I think Otis Redding was there one night,

999
00:46:54.565 --> 00:46:56.391
and James Brown came one night.

1000
00:46:56.391 --> 00:46:58.217
Oh yeah, they would put on

1001
00:46:58.217 --> 00:46:59.739
<c.p>some big dances over there.

1002
00:46:59.739 --> 00:47:03.087
And my momma . . . like I say my momma ran the café.

1003
00:47:03.087 --> 00:47:08.565
She was the head over the café and her and my daddy would run the café at night.

1004
00:47:08.565 --> 00:47:11.000
It was . . . Honey that was good then.

1005
00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:11.920
<v ARSENEAULT>She worked late?

1006
00:47:11.920 --> 00:47:12.534
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, yeah.

1007
00:47:12.534 --> 00:47:18.364
They would work late, run that café, but at that time when they ran the café it wasn’t bad.

1008
00:47:18.364 --> 00:47:22.966
People didn’t bother you back then because we’ll leave the front door open like this.

1009
00:47:22.966 --> 00:47:27.875
The front door open we’ll go to sleep, or the windows open we’ll go to sleep.

1010
00:47:27.875 --> 00:47:29.102
Nobody would bother you.

1011
00:47:29.102 --> 00:47:30.943
We didn’t have all of that.

1012
00:47:30.943 --> 00:47:36.159
Now you leave your doors open, you ain’t got nothing you got . . . They going to rob you.

1013
00:47:36.159 --> 00:47:41.989
But that’s how we slept and it was cool at night and the weather back there was more colder.

1014
00:47:41.989 --> 00:47:46.284
I remember icicles used to hang off the house, the roof of the house,

1015
00:47:46.284 --> 00:47:50.273
and we used to walk on the ground and just crunch crunch crunch.

1016
00:47:50.273 --> 00:47:51.807
We don’t have that anymore.

1017
00:47:51.807 --> 00:47:54.261
We don’t have that kind of weather anymore.

1018
00:47:54.261 --> 00:47:56.102
So we had . . . it was good.

1019
00:47:56.102 --> 00:47:58.557
Like I say Mr. Valery he was good.

1020
00:47:58.557 --> 00:48:03.773
Mostly you didn’t see him too much it was always his wife Mrs. Eula, we’ll see her.

1021
00:48:03.773 --> 00:48:05.000
We’ll see her.

1022
00:48:05.000 --> 00:48:07.025
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about her?

1023
00:48:07.025 --> 00:48:09.725
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord Mrs. Eula, she was so beautiful.

1024
00:48:09.725 --> 00:48:10.400
Beautiful complected.

1025
00:48:10.400 --> 00:48:15.125
She just . . . She was like Momma just canned and just taking care of kids.

1026
00:48:15.125 --> 00:48:16.475
That’s how she was.

1027
00:48:16.475 --> 00:48:25.250
She was strict because I remember Judy and then my brother would say how strict she was with the girls because the girls were so beautiful.

1028
00:48:25.250 --> 00:48:27.275
Them girls was some beautiful girls.

1029
00:48:27.275 --> 00:48:28.962
And she would be so

1030
00:48:28.962 --> 00:48:32.000
<c.p>strict on them because them boys would go there.

1031
00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:34.025
She’ll run them away, “Go somewhere.”

1032
00:48:34.025 --> 00:48:35.375
But they was beautiful.

1033
00:48:35.375 --> 00:48:39.425
But like I say she was just like my mom and them.

1034
00:48:39.425 --> 00:48:42.800
She just . . . hey, did what she had to do, too.

1035
00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:49.550
Canned and then she’ll call my momma for my brothers them come help pick figs, pears and stuff for her.

1036
00:48:49.550 --> 00:48:50.900
They did all that.

1037
00:48:50.900 --> 00:48:52.250
That’s how we did.

1038
00:48:52.250 --> 00:48:56.975
We helped the neighbors, the neighbors kids helped us, so that’s how we did.

1039
00:48:56.975 --> 00:48:59.000
So we had good times.

1040
00:48:59.000 --> 00:49:01.500
<v ARSENEAULT>What about Mr. Valery’s politics?

1041
00:49:01.500 --> 00:49:02.500
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord.

1042
00:49:02.500 --> 00:49:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about that?

1043
00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:06.840
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord.

1044
00:49:06.840 --> 00:49:14.820
Baby when he speak you please believe you everybody was voting for whoever he was saying to vote for.

1045
00:49:14.820 --> 00:49:19.860
Because he was right on the money and that’s how he was.

1046
00:49:19.860 --> 00:49:21.960
And he run that campaign.

1047
00:49:21.960 --> 00:49:27.000
He was a . . . was it sheriff . . . what was that sheriff’s name?

1048
00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:27.639
<v ARSENEAULT>Ham Reed?

1049
00:49:27.639 --> 00:49:28.277
<v HUNTSBERRY>Ham Reed.

1050
00:49:28.277 --> 00:49:28.916
Lord, Jesus.

1051
00:49:28.916 --> 00:49:32.108
He’ll tell you quick that’s the man for the job.

1052
00:49:32.108 --> 00:49:33.066
That’s the man.

1053
00:49:33.066 --> 00:49:38.175
And he’ll run and he’ll come sit to you, “I need you to vote for him.”

1054
00:49:38.175 --> 00:49:40.090
And he will push you, too.

1055
00:49:40.090 --> 00:49:41.687
But he did some stuff.

1056
00:49:41.687 --> 00:49:42.964
He did some stuff.

1057
00:49:42.964 --> 00:49:45.837
We used to laugh at him all the time.

1058
00:49:45.837 --> 00:49:48.072
We call him the campaign person too.

1059
00:49:48.072 --> 00:49:49.030
Here he come.

1060
00:49:49.030 --> 00:49:51.584
Now who you want us to vote for

1061
00:49:51.584 --> 00:49:51.904
<c.p>now?

1062
00:49:51.904 --> 00:49:57.012
That’s what he . . . Momma and them used to tell him that time because we was young.

1063
00:49:57.012 --> 00:49:58.289
We didn’t vote then.

1064
00:49:58.289 --> 00:49:59.566
And it was funny.

1065
00:49:59.566 --> 00:50:00.524
It was funny.

1066
00:50:00.524 --> 00:50:02.759
He did a whole lot of stuff.

1067
00:50:02.759 --> 00:50:07.229
He even had a company in the plant that . . . him and his brother Wes.

1068
00:50:07.229 --> 00:50:09.783
Wes stay down the street right here now,

1069
00:50:09.783 --> 00:50:15.530
Wes . . . they had a company in the plant where they had them vacuum trucks and stuff like that.

1070
00:50:15.530 --> 00:50:17.127
So they did a lot.

1071
00:50:17.127 --> 00:50:18.404
They did a lot.

1072
00:50:18.404 --> 00:50:20.000
They did a lot.

1073
00:50:20.000 --> 00:50:22.625
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about Mr. Wes?

1074
00:50:22.625 --> 00:50:23.750
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, that’s Daddy.

1075
00:50:23.750 --> 00:50:25.250
That’s my second daddy.

1076
00:50:25.250 --> 00:50:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Really?

1077
00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:26.577
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, yeah.

1078
00:50:26.577 --> 00:50:28.019
That’s my second daddy there.

1079
00:50:28.019 --> 00:50:36.962
He was . . . like I said we all . . . and that’s how we is with his children and we call each other sisters . . . and we not really related we call each other sisters.

1080
00:50:36.962 --> 00:50:41.288
And even today we call each other sisters because that’s how close we was raised.

1081
00:50:41.288 --> 00:50:48.500
We either was at her house, I was at his house, sewing with [Terrella?], or Terrella would come sew, or show me how to sew.

1082
00:50:48.500 --> 00:50:55.712
Or if she had some material left over she’ll bring it to me, and if I have some material left I’ll bring it to her.

1083
00:50:55.712 --> 00:50:57.154
And that’s how we did.

1084
00:50:57.154 --> 00:50:59.173
That’s how . . . we had our clothes, so.

1085
00:50:59.173 --> 00:51:00.327
But he was nice.

1086
00:51:00.327 --> 00:51:02.923
He . . . like I said he worked at the plants.

1087
00:51:02.923 --> 00:51:06.962
And then him and Mrs. Agnes before she died, bless her heart, his wife,

1088
00:51:06.962 --> 00:51:11.865
they opened up a club called the Rolls Royce and I used to be a waitress there.

1089
00:51:11.865 --> 00:51:17.635
It was a family . . . and everybody got mad because people couldn’t work there because they say it’s too much family.

1090
00:51:17.635 --> 00:51:18.500
And it was.

1091
00:51:18.500 --> 00:51:22.538
It was like a family [rely?] on . . . we just . . . The family just worked there.

1092
00:51:22.538 --> 00:51:23.981
And we’ll work there and

1093
00:51:23.981 --> 00:51:26.000
<c.p>we had fun until it closed.

1094
00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:27.388
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it in Mossville?

1095
00:51:27.388 --> 00:51:29.816
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Disagrees] It was here in Lake Charles.

1096
00:51:29.816 --> 00:51:34.327
They had the club here in Lake Charles and we would work it.

1097
00:51:34.327 --> 00:51:35.020
Oh, yeah.

1098
00:51:35.020 --> 00:51:36.408
So we had fun.

1099
00:51:36.408 --> 00:51:38.837
Don’t let them get to that camera.

1100
00:51:38.837 --> 00:51:40.224
Don’t let . . . hey, stop.

1101
00:51:40.224 --> 00:51:43.000
Yeah so, but we had fun, Mr. . . .

1102
00:51:43.000 --> 00:51:45.520
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember about his dad?

1103
00:51:45.520 --> 00:51:45.940
<v HUNTSBERRY>No.

1104
00:51:45.940 --> 00:51:47.200
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Valery’s dad?

1105
00:51:47.200 --> 00:51:50.560
<v HUNTSBERRY>No. [Disagrees] No, I don’t remember his dad.

1106
00:51:50.560 --> 00:51:55.600
But I think they came from down east if I’m thinking . . . Yeah,

1107
00:51:55.600 --> 00:52:01.060
they came from down east, but I never remember his dad and stuff.

1108
00:52:01.060 --> 00:52:03.160
Did they do one too?

1109
00:52:03.160 --> 00:52:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Hmm?

1110
00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:05.862
<v HUNTSBERRY>Did they do an interview, too?

1111
00:52:05.862 --> 00:52:06.172
Judy?

1112
00:52:06.172 --> 00:52:07.724
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Valery was too sick.

1113
00:52:07.724 --> 00:52:09.586
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah he was in the hospital.

1114
00:52:09.586 --> 00:52:12.069
<v ARSENEAULT>So I’m trying to ask around about him.

1115
00:52:12.069 --> 00:52:13.310
I interviewed Ms. Judy.

1116
00:52:13.310 --> 00:52:13.621
<v HUNTSBERRY>Okay.

1117
00:52:13.621 --> 00:52:19.517
<v ARSENEAULT>And she talked a lot about her dad, and I was sad I didn’t get to talk to him.

1118
00:52:19.517 --> 00:52:19.828
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah.

1119
00:52:19.828 --> 00:52:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I think he’s too sick, so.

1120
00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:24.672
<v HUNTSBERRY>It’s amazing how he got that Alzheimer’s.

1121
00:52:24.672 --> 00:52:35.361
He took sick and Mrs. Eula would take care of him and here we is thinking Mrs. Eula was healthy and she wound up dying before he did.

1122
00:52:35.361 --> 00:52:36.889
So it was unreal.

1123
00:52:36.889 --> 00:52:38.034
It was unreal.

1124
00:52:38.034 --> 00:52:39.179
It was unreal.

1125
00:52:39.179 --> 00:52:41.088
Yeah he’s very sick there.

1126
00:52:41.088 --> 00:52:42.233
Bless his heart.

1127
00:52:42.233 --> 00:52:44.142
He’s just like my dad.

1128
00:52:44.142 --> 00:52:52.159
My dad knows a lot too, but he got that lung cancer now so he’s short of breath now and stuff.

1129
00:52:52.159 --> 00:52:55.595
<v ARSENEAULT>What about who the Rigmaiden Center is named for?

1130
00:52:55.595 --> 00:52:58.649
Where they . . . did you grow up with them?

1131
00:52:58.649 --> 00:52:59.030
James?

1132
00:52:59.030 --> 00:53:00.557
<v HUNTSBERRY>James Rigmaiden and them?

1133
00:53:00.557 --> 00:53:00.939
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

1134
00:53:00.939 --> 00:53:02.848
<v HUNTSBERRY>With his kids I did.

1135
00:53:02.848 --> 00:53:08.193
I came up with them and they named after . . . Mr. James I think died.

1136
00:53:08.193 --> 00:53:13.155
They named that . . . I remember that, but other than that I don’t know.

1137
00:53:13.155 --> 00:53:19.645
We went to school with the Rigmaidens at school in Mossville and that’s how I knew them.

1138
00:53:19.645 --> 00:53:27.662
But like I said they was just . . . I wouldn’t say to they self, we was just too busy working at home.

1139
00:53:27.662 --> 00:53:29.571
That’s what we did, so.

1140
00:53:29.571 --> 00:53:34.534
And like I said I see them more when we was at school.

1141
00:53:34.534 --> 00:53:35.679
That was it.

1142
00:53:35.679 --> 00:53:39.115
But I didn’t know too much about Mr. James.

1143
00:53:39.115 --> 00:53:45.986
Now his wife Mrs. Lola Bell, she got the funeral home right here, a blessing that woman is.

1144
00:53:45.986 --> 00:53:50.186
Every time you see her you see her as she is.

1145
00:53:50.186 --> 00:53:52.095
She never change her feature.

1146
00:53:52.095 --> 00:53:55.149
She’s always got a smile on her face.

1147
00:53:55.149 --> 00:54:03.929
And she will go out of her way and try and help you and that was . . . and even today that’s how she is.

1148
00:54:03.929 --> 00:54:05.074
She’ll help you.

1149
00:54:05.074 --> 00:54:09.655
She’ll help you and . . . but other than that I didn’t know him.

1150
00:54:09.655 --> 00:54:13.091
Like I said Mr. James, I didn’t know him.

1151
00:54:13.091 --> 00:54:15.000
I never knew him.

1152
00:54:15.000 --> 00:54:22.574
<v ARSENEAULT>Did the . . . did you ever hear the elder people talking about a post office that was in Mossville or anything like that?

1153
00:54:22.574 --> 00:54:26.361
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Disagrees] no. No the post office I remember is in Westlake.

1154
00:54:26.361 --> 00:54:29.459
I didn’t . . . because our stuff would come from Westlake.

1155
00:54:29.459 --> 00:54:30.836
Not a post office.

1156
00:54:30.836 --> 00:54:33.246
We had a lot of clubs, now.

1157
00:54:33.246 --> 00:54:34.967
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the clubs?

1158
00:54:34.967 --> 00:54:36.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord.

1159
00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:37.967
<v ARSENEAULT>Were they all locally owned?

1160
00:54:37.967 --> 00:54:38.361
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah.

1161
00:54:38.361 --> 00:54:38.754
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1162
00:54:38.754 --> 00:54:42.295
<v HUNTSBERRY>The Cowboy Club was in Bel Air in Mossville.

1163
00:54:42.295 --> 00:54:49.770
And then we had . . . Dog Hill was on Highway 90 way back in them days that was on 90.

1164
00:54:49.770 --> 00:54:57.639
And like I said the Paradise Club and then they had another club I don’t remember the name of it.

1165
00:54:57.639 --> 00:55:03.934
It was a little further down like you finna to go . . . In that curve in Mossville.

1166
00:55:03.934 --> 00:55:07.082
Lord, what was the name of that club?

1167
00:55:07.082 --> 00:55:15.344
I don’t remember but we had canteens and sometime Momma would run the canteen when we put on parties and stuff.

1168
00:55:15.344 --> 00:55:22.033
We had parties there, or house parties, we had house parties or we’ll go to the canteen.

1169
00:55:22.033 --> 00:55:24.000
We had that, but . . .

1170
00:55:24.000 --> 00:55:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s a canteen?

1171
00:55:25.000 --> 00:55:27.333
<v HUNTSBERRY>A canteen is just like a hall.

1172
00:55:27.333 --> 00:55:31.667
It was a hall and they . . . Back then they called it a canteen.

1173
00:55:31.667 --> 00:55:33.000
And it was a . . .

1174
00:55:33.000 --> 00:55:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it for the young people?

1175
00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:36.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, yeah.

1176
00:55:36.000 --> 00:55:36.385
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1177
00:55:36.385 --> 00:55:37.538
<v HUNTSBERRY>Young or old.

1178
00:55:37.538 --> 00:55:42.154
Whoever putting the dance on and that was just a good time.

1179
00:55:42.154 --> 00:55:44.462
So but that’s we basically did.

1180
00:55:44.462 --> 00:55:49.462
Well basically we had . . . Basically most of the time we had house parties.

1181
00:55:49.462 --> 00:55:51.385
We had house parties, so.

1182
00:55:51.385 --> 00:55:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would you do at the house party?

1183
00:55:56.000 --> 00:55:57.273
<v HUNTSBERRY>For what, the parties?

1184
00:55:57.273 --> 00:55:57.591
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

1185
00:55:57.591 --> 00:55:59.818
<v HUNTSBERRY>Nothing . . . Sit down look at people dance.

1186
00:55:59.818 --> 00:56:00.455
I danced.

1187
00:56:00.455 --> 00:56:02.045
Oh, I loved to dance.

1188
00:56:02.045 --> 00:56:03.000
Oh, yeah.

1189
00:56:03.000 --> 00:56:04.386
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of dances?

1190
00:56:04.386 --> 00:56:05.426
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord girl.

1191
00:56:05.426 --> 00:56:08.199
Remember them dances in them days? [Laughs] Wow.

1192
00:56:08.199 --> 00:56:09.932
I don’t remember them dances.

1193
00:56:09.932 --> 00:56:11.665
Honey we used to dance.

1194
00:56:11.665 --> 00:56:13.745
That’s all I could tell you.

1195
00:56:13.745 --> 00:56:16.171
I loved to dance at that time.

1196
00:56:16.171 --> 00:56:17.211
I . . . even at . . .

1197
00:56:17.211 --> 00:56:18.944
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it with a partner?

1198
00:56:18.944 --> 00:56:19.984
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, yeah we’ll . . .

1199
00:56:19.984 --> 00:56:21.024
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s with partners.

1200
00:56:21.024 --> 00:56:27.610
<v HUNTSBERRY>Partners or group, or however, but then we . . . at Mossville we’ll put on what they call a talent show.

1201
00:56:27.610 --> 00:56:29.689
And baby . . . Momma said, “Oh, Lord.”

1202
00:56:29.689 --> 00:56:33.155
I said, “You know I’m going for the go-go girl.”

1203
00:56:33.155 --> 00:56:39.048
And I always go for the go-go girls and we’ll open the show and close the show.

1204
00:56:39.048 --> 00:56:41.474
It was like ten, fifteen of us.

1205
00:56:41.474 --> 00:56:42.861
And we’ll do that.

1206
00:56:42.861 --> 00:56:45.633
I said, “Oh I had to get it.”

1207
00:56:45.633 --> 00:56:47.020
I had to go.

1208
00:56:47.020 --> 00:56:48.060
We did that.

1209
00:56:48.060 --> 00:56:57.072
And a lot of that . . . A lot of the entertainer that was what we put on at school that people coming on Fridays night and see.

1210
00:56:57.072 --> 00:56:59.498
But we did a lot of that.

1211
00:56:59.498 --> 00:57:02.964
We did a lot of that coming up in Mossville.

1212
00:57:02.964 --> 00:57:07.470
So that was like in the late part of the 60s going . . . 70s.

1213
00:57:07.470 --> 00:57:07.817
Yeah.

1214
00:57:07.817 --> 00:57:08.857
We’ll do that.

1215
00:57:08.857 --> 00:57:09.896
We’ll do that.

1216
00:57:09.896 --> 00:57:10.936
We did that.

1217
00:57:10.936 --> 00:57:15.442
But other than that besides the football team . . . My brother played football there.

1218
00:57:15.442 --> 00:57:20.988
And my other brother was a drum major for one year I think it was, Jerry.

1219
00:57:20.988 --> 00:57:26.534
So Mossville was a big big school and it was . . . That’s all I can tell you.

1220
00:57:26.534 --> 00:57:27.574
We had fun.

1221
00:57:27.574 --> 00:57:28.614
We had fun.

1222
00:57:28.614 --> 00:57:30.000
We had fun.

1223
00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:31.364
<v ARSENEAULT>So you started first grade . . .?

1224
00:57:31.364 --> 00:57:31.909
<v HUNTSBERRY>First grade.

1225
00:57:31.909 --> 00:57:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>At Mossville Elementary?

1226
00:57:33.000 --> 00:57:33.267
<v HUNTSBERRY>Elementary.

1227
00:57:33.267 --> 00:57:35.138
Well I was at Mossville High School.

1228
00:57:35.138 --> 00:57:36.741
<v ARSENEAULT>So it had all the grades?

1229
00:57:36.741 --> 00:57:39.147
<v HUNTSBERRY>It was all the . . . it had all the grades.

1230
00:57:39.147 --> 00:57:43.422
It was from first to twelfth at that time because I remember in the . . . fourth grade?

1231
00:57:43.422 --> 00:57:46.362
I used to . . . the teacher used to holler at my brother,

1232
00:57:46.362 --> 00:57:49.836
“Why you giving that girl them big old heavy books to bring home?”

1233
00:57:49.836 --> 00:57:52.509
And my brother would come and say, “Bring this home.

1234
00:57:52.509 --> 00:57:53.845
Bring this home for me.”

1235
00:57:53.845 --> 00:57:59.457
Because he had football practice and I used to have to bring them heavy books of his at home for him.

1236
00:57:59.457 --> 00:58:04.000
And yeah, yeah it was from first grade to twelfth grade when I started over there.

1237
00:58:04.000 --> 00:58:05.590
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you walk to school?

1238
00:58:05.590 --> 00:58:07.498
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, no. We rode the bus.

1239
00:58:07.498 --> 00:58:12.267
Ms. [Earline?] . . . what was her name . . . Broussard . . . Ms. Earline and Mr. . . . What was his name?

1240
00:58:12.267 --> 00:58:12.585
Broussard?

1241
00:58:12.585 --> 00:58:13.857
No, it wasn’t Broussard.

1242
00:58:13.857 --> 00:58:16.401
It was Ms. Earline was our bus driver.

1243
00:58:16.401 --> 00:58:17.991
And she did not play.

1244
00:58:17.991 --> 00:58:21.171
You wasn’t at that bus when she pull up there,

1245
00:58:21.171 --> 00:58:28.484
and if you was running to the bus she was going to leave you because you should have been at that bus stop.

1246
00:58:28.484 --> 00:58:30.392
And that’s how Ms. Earline was.

1247
00:58:30.392 --> 00:58:31.346
She didn’t play.

1248
00:58:31.346 --> 00:58:38.341
And if them kids cut up on that bus, she’ll stop the bus on the side the road and put them out.

1249
00:58:38.341 --> 00:58:39.613
She’ll put them out.

1250
00:58:39.613 --> 00:58:40.885
Make them walk home.

1251
00:58:40.885 --> 00:58:48.516
And the next day you were in that principal office and the principal would take you home and say this is what you did.

1252
00:58:48.516 --> 00:58:50.106
And that’s what they did.

1253
00:58:50.106 --> 00:58:54.240
The teachers would . . . Back then would bring you home if you cut up.

1254
00:58:54.240 --> 00:58:58.373
They would bring you home and you knew you had a whooping then.

1255
00:58:58.373 --> 00:58:59.645
You had a whooping.

1256
00:58:59.645 --> 00:59:03.143
But that’s how it was when we came up in school.

1257
00:59:03.143 --> 00:59:06.959
Baby in . . . we didn’t . . . I didn’t . . . like I said I was quiet.

1258
00:59:06.959 --> 00:59:08.548
I was real quiet, so.

1259
00:59:08.548 --> 00:59:11.410
I see a lot, but I was quiet, so.

1260
00:59:11.410 --> 00:59:13.000
But it was good.

1261
00:59:13.000 --> 00:59:15.476
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have any favorite teachers?

1262
00:59:15.476 --> 00:59:17.952
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, Lord it was Mr. Mouton.

1263
00:59:17.952 --> 00:59:19.190
Bless his heart.

1264
00:59:19.190 --> 00:59:20.841
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s his first name?

1265
00:59:20.841 --> 00:59:22.079
Do you remember?

1266
00:59:22.079 --> 00:59:22.905
<v HUNTSBERRY>Johnny Mouton.

1267
00:59:22.905 --> 00:59:24.968
He was our math teacher.

1268
00:59:24.968 --> 00:59:30.746
And Mr. Mouton and Ms. . . . what was Ms. [Sacheries?] . . . she was an English teacher.

1269
00:59:30.746 --> 00:59:36.111
Ms. [Sacherie?] was our . . . was another teacher I had that I really loved.

1270
00:59:36.111 --> 00:59:39.413
And Ms. Franklin, she was over the library.

1271
00:59:39.413 --> 00:59:49.317
I loved Ms. Franklin because I worked at the library when I was in school so I had a good bonding with Ms. Franklin.

1272
00:59:49.317 --> 00:59:54.683
Matter of fact, we still talk on Facebook so . . . Me and Ms. Franklin.

1273
00:59:54.683 --> 00:59:57.984
And oh yeah it was some good days.

1274
00:59:57.984 --> 01:00:00.460
Ms. Reynolds . . . Oh, Lord Ms. Reynolds.

1275
01:00:00.460 --> 01:00:02.524
That was the bomb there.

1276
01:00:02.524 --> 01:00:03.349
Ms. Reynolds.

1277
01:00:03.349 --> 01:00:05.000
She’s sick now.

1278
01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:06.975
<v ARSENEAULT>What did she teach?

1279
01:00:06.975 --> 01:00:12.899
<v HUNTSBERRY>She teached . . . what did Ms. Reynolds . . . I was in her home room,

1280
01:00:12.899 --> 01:00:19.810
but I think she teach American history I think it was at the time.

1281
01:00:19.810 --> 01:00:26.228
I think she taught American history, but I was in her home room.

1282
01:00:26.228 --> 01:00:27.709
And oh, Lord.

1283
01:00:27.709 --> 01:00:30.177
Sweet as she could be.

1284
01:00:30.177 --> 01:00:32.646
Sweet as she could be.

1285
01:00:32.646 --> 01:00:33.633
Ms. Reynolds.

1286
01:00:33.633 --> 01:00:35.114
I loved her.

1287
01:00:35.114 --> 01:00:37.089
That’s basically about it.

1288
01:00:37.089 --> 01:00:41.532
The ones that I stuck closer to was them.

1289
01:00:41.532 --> 01:00:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What happened with integration?

1290
01:00:44.000 --> 01:00:45.980
<v HUNTSBERRY>When we went to Sulphur High?

1291
01:00:45.980 --> 01:00:49.279
It was a . . . when I got there it was okay.

1292
01:00:49.279 --> 01:00:53.569
See you had like four classes before me, before I . . . I don’t know,

1293
01:00:53.569 --> 01:00:57.859
I had went with them, but when I go there I was fine.

1294
01:00:57.859 --> 01:00:58.848
We was fine.

1295
01:00:58.848 --> 01:01:01.158
I had a lot of white friends.

1296
01:01:01.158 --> 01:01:04.458
Matter of fact some of them hanged out with us,

1297
01:01:04.458 --> 01:01:12.047
some of them we took to the club with us to show them how we got down when we went to Sulphur High.

1298
01:01:12.047 --> 01:01:13.037
We was fine.

1299
01:01:13.037 --> 01:01:16.997
There was like 458 of us when I graduated from Sulphur High.

1300
01:01:16.997 --> 01:01:18.316
So we was fine.

1301
01:01:18.316 --> 01:01:23.596
It wasn’t . . . it got bad maybe one time I was at Sulphur it got real bad.

1302
01:01:23.596 --> 01:01:25.906
That’s the time Roots first came out.

1303
01:01:25.906 --> 01:01:29.205
And the boys . . . we would set on the stairs, us.

1304
01:01:29.205 --> 01:01:37.785
The blacks would set on the stairs and the other kids would be like in the canteen . . . Black and white would be at the canteen, whatever.

1305
01:01:37.785 --> 01:01:41.744
But we mostly sat on the stairs before the school take in.

1306
01:01:41.744 --> 01:01:47.354
And I think they passed though there calling us the n-word and I said, “Oh my God.”

1307
01:01:47.354 --> 01:01:52.963
And baby the next morning they came through saying it again and they got they butt whooped.

1308
01:01:52.963 --> 01:01:54.283
And that was it.

1309
01:01:54.283 --> 01:01:57.253
And Principal Moses said, “We ain’t having that here.”

1310
01:01:57.253 --> 01:02:02.202
And he got on the loudspeaker and he put that down right then and there.

1311
01:02:02.202 --> 01:02:05.172
He said, “We’re not going to have that here.”

1312
01:02:05.172 --> 01:02:08.141
Other than that, after that, it was good sailing.

1313
01:02:08.141 --> 01:02:09.131
I got . . . Lord,

1314
01:02:09.131 --> 01:02:18.370
I was . . . matter of fact I was looking at some pictures the other day from some of the stuff we did at school over there at Sulphur High.

1315
01:02:18.370 --> 01:02:19.690
But it was okay.

1316
01:02:19.690 --> 01:02:20.680
It was okay.

1317
01:02:20.680 --> 01:02:22.000
It was okay.

1318
01:02:22.000 --> 01:02:28.109
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any protests or anything maybe in Lake Charles or Westlake maybe?

1319
01:02:28.109 --> 01:02:29.855
<v HUNTSBERRY>Mossville . . . It wasn’t Rougeau’s.

1320
01:02:29.855 --> 01:02:32.036
Was it . . . Was Trahan’s maybe?

1321
01:02:32.036 --> 01:02:36.400
Or Rougeau . . . It was a store down on Prater Road.

1322
01:02:36.400 --> 01:02:38.582
I don’t know what happened.

1323
01:02:38.582 --> 01:02:39.891
My brother could

1324
01:02:39.891 --> 01:02:41.636
<c.p>tell y’all that one.

1325
01:02:41.636 --> 01:02:43.818
I don’t know what happened.

1326
01:02:43.818 --> 01:02:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Were they protesting Rougeau’s?

1327
01:02:46.000 --> 01:02:48.887
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah they protest and it . . . I don’t know what . . .

1328
01:02:48.887 --> 01:02:50.170
<v ARSENEAULT>I heard about that.

1329
01:02:50.170 --> 01:02:51.774
<v HUNTSBERRY>I don’t know what happened.

1330
01:02:51.774 --> 01:02:56.264
They protest that and my brother wind up . . . they wind up going to jail.

1331
01:02:56.264 --> 01:03:02.358
All of them did, I think the majority of them, so my dad then had to go get them.

1332
01:03:02.358 --> 01:03:04.283
But I don’t know what happened.

1333
01:03:04.283 --> 01:03:09.415
I can call him and ask him what happened at Rougeau’s for them to start protesting.

1334
01:03:09.415 --> 01:03:11.981
I don’t know, but I knew they did.

1335
01:03:11.981 --> 01:03:13.264
I knew they did.

1336
01:03:13.264 --> 01:03:15.509
Like I told you I was quiet.

1337
01:03:15.509 --> 01:03:16.472
I stayed home.

1338
01:03:16.472 --> 01:03:18.717
But I remember they talked about it.

1339
01:03:18.717 --> 01:03:20.000
But I can . . .

1340
01:03:20.000 --> 01:03:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So what year were you born?

1341
01:03:22.000 --> 01:03:22.333
<v HUNTSBERRY>1960.

1342
01:03:22.333 --> 01:03:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You were born in 1960?

1343
01:03:24.000 --> 01:03:24.333
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Agrees].

1344
01:03:24.333 --> 01:03:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1345
01:03:25.000 --> 01:03:29.928
<v HUNTSBERRY>And that’s why a lot of people say, “You were born in 1960 and how you remember?”

1346
01:03:29.928 --> 01:03:31.667
I remember a lot of stuff.

1347
01:03:31.667 --> 01:03:32.246
I can.

1348
01:03:32.246 --> 01:03:37.174
And a lot of stuff I research and a lot of stuff I ask other people about.

1349
01:03:37.174 --> 01:03:38.333
I did some research.

1350
01:03:38.333 --> 01:03:41.232
<v ARSENEAULT>I think the 60s were kind of a crazy time.

1351
01:03:41.232 --> 01:03:43.841
President Kennedy was getting assassinated, Doctor King was getting . . .

1352
01:03:43.841 --> 01:03:45.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah assassinated [agrees].

1353
01:03:45.000 --> 01:03:46.774
<v ARSENEAULT>Remember anything about that?

1354
01:03:46.774 --> 01:03:48.991
<v HUNTSBERRY>No because I was young.

1355
01:03:48.991 --> 01:03:53.870
I was . . . [talking about her grandson] Oh, Lord he’s getting close.

1356
01:03:53.870 --> 01:03:55.643
<v ARSENEAULT>He wants that microphone.

1357
01:03:55.643 --> 01:03:58.748
I see him looking at that microphone.

1358
01:03:58.748 --> 01:04:04.070
<v HUNTSBERRY>No because like I said I was very young at that time.

1359
01:04:04.070 --> 01:04:08.948
And like I said people didn’t talk about stuff like that.

1360
01:04:08.948 --> 01:04:12.496
They didn’t really talk in front of us.

1361
01:04:12.496 --> 01:04:16.043
They really didn’t talk about stuff like that.

1362
01:04:16.043 --> 01:04:16.930
They didn’t.

1363
01:04:16.930 --> 01:04:23.139
So the people that like I said my mom friends was . . . they didn’t talk.

1364
01:04:23.139 --> 01:04:24.470
They didn’t talk.

1365
01:04:24.470 --> 01:04:29.791
And my aunties and them they didn’t talk about stuff like that.

1366
01:04:29.791 --> 01:04:32.452
Not in front of us, so.

1367
01:04:32.452 --> 01:04:36.000
Some things we just didn’t know, so.

1368
01:04:36.000 --> 01:04:37.032
<v ARSENEAULT>Let’s see.

1369
01:04:37.032 --> 01:04:40.645
So, local businesses . . . some local . . . I know

1370
01:04:40.645 --> 01:04:41.677
<c.p>there’s Rougeau’s.

1371
01:04:41.677 --> 01:04:46.323
<v HUNTSBERRY>There was Rougeau’s and they had Midwest drive through.

1372
01:04:46.323 --> 01:04:56.645
It was a movie . . . it was . . . you go in your cars and park your cars and look at the movies.

1373
01:04:56.645 --> 01:04:59.742
Like remember when they had that?

1374
01:04:59.742 --> 01:05:01.290
They had that.

1375
01:05:01.290 --> 01:05:07.484
They had the Paradise, no they had the Pelican Café down there.

1376
01:05:07.484 --> 01:05:11.613
They had the old Chateaux Charles down there.

1377
01:05:11.613 --> 01:05:18.839
They had a lot of burger places back then but the names have changed.

1378
01:05:18.839 --> 01:05:20.903
I can’t remember but . . .

1379
01:05:20.903 --> 01:05:22.452
<v ARSENEAULT>Like little food . . .?

1380
01:05:22.452 --> 01:05:24.000
Like food . . .

1381
01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:26.194
<v HUNTSBERRY>That fast food stuff like that.

1382
01:05:26.194 --> 01:05:26.925
<v ARSENEAULT>Fast food.

1383
01:05:26.925 --> 01:05:31.677
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah like I said because my momma worked on a lot of them.

1384
01:05:31.677 --> 01:05:36.796
When she was younger she worked a lot of them fast food stuff . . . restaurants.

1385
01:05:36.796 --> 01:05:37.892
She did that.

1386
01:05:37.892 --> 01:05:41.914
She also ironed for some elderly people they was in Maplewood.

1387
01:05:41.914 --> 01:05:43.011
Some white ladies [. . .?].

1388
01:05:43.011 --> 01:05:50.323
Her and some of her friends would go down there and do some ironing . . . make they little change from there.

1389
01:05:50.323 --> 01:05:58.000
And besides selling . . . Like I said they did a lot of selling blackberries and pecans and stuff that they picked.

1390
01:05:58.000 --> 01:05:58.933
<v ARSENEAULT>They sold it?

1391
01:05:58.933 --> 01:06:02.667
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah they sold that on side of the road, stuff like that.

1392
01:06:02.667 --> 01:06:04.222
They did all of that.

1393
01:06:04.222 --> 01:06:05.778
They did all of that.

1394
01:06:05.778 --> 01:06:08.267
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know how much they’d charged for . . .?

1395
01:06:08.267 --> 01:06:09.511
<v HUNTSBERRY>Fifty cents a can.

1396
01:06:09.511 --> 01:06:10.756
Fifty cents a can.

1397
01:06:10.756 --> 01:06:12.000
And that’s when . . .

1398
01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:14.609
<v ARSENEAULT>Now it’s like ten dollars a can . . .

1399
01:06:14.609 --> 01:06:14.982
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah.

1400
01:06:14.982 --> 01:06:20.574
And so like Joe was . . . but back then things wasn’t expensive as it is now.

1401
01:06:20.574 --> 01:06:24.675
Fifty cents go a long way back then and it did.

1402
01:06:24.675 --> 01:06:41.822
And that’s why Joe was telling one of the ladies told him Momma had sold . . . when she sold her blackberries . . . because that’s why I say Joe wanted to ask her and he didn’t ask her how much she made because she bought her first winter coat.

1403
01:06:41.822 --> 01:06:47.787
Her first winter coat from that by selling them . . . Picking blackberries and pecans and selling them.

1404
01:06:47.787 --> 01:06:49.278
And they did that.

1405
01:06:49.278 --> 01:06:50.396
They did that.

1406
01:06:50.396 --> 01:06:53.379
And we had a lot of pecan trees.

1407
01:06:53.379 --> 01:06:57.107
I remember a whole lot of pecan trees in Mossville.

1408
01:06:57.107 --> 01:07:01.953
Anywhere you go you find pecans, and blackberries, and mayhaw trees and stuff.

1409
01:07:01.953 --> 01:07:04.935
Now you go and you can’t find nothing.

1410
01:07:04.935 --> 01:07:06.053
Can’t find nothing.

1411
01:07:06.053 --> 01:07:09.781
They had a whole lot of that was going on.

1412
01:07:09.781 --> 01:07:11.272
And they did that.

1413
01:07:11.272 --> 01:07:12.763
That’s how they survived.

1414
01:07:12.763 --> 01:07:15.000
That’s how they survived, so.

1415
01:07:15.000 --> 01:07:17.567
<v ARSENEAULT>And your . . . and Pinky was a midwife?

1416
01:07:17.567 --> 01:07:19.033
<v HUNTSBERRY>Pinky was a midwife

1417
01:07:19.033 --> 01:07:24.533
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know what she did, or did she ever talk about it or anything?

1418
01:07:24.533 --> 01:07:28.567
<v HUNTSBERRY>No because me and Veda, her granddaughter would talk about it.

1419
01:07:28.567 --> 01:07:32.233
How she would go deliver babies and stuff like that.

1420
01:07:32.233 --> 01:07:37.000
And she never would say what happened to the babies or whatever . . .

1421
01:07:37.000 --> 01:07:41.455
<v ARSENEAULT>They just had all had them in the home at that time?

1422
01:07:41.455 --> 01:07:41.826
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah.

1423
01:07:41.826 --> 01:07:43.311
All in the homes.

1424
01:07:43.311 --> 01:07:44.795
All in the home.

1425
01:07:44.795 --> 01:07:50.364
And like I say cause they was back here in slavery time, Grandma Pinky’s mom,

1426
01:07:50.364 --> 01:08:00.386
because her . . . I was telling my brother I wish I would have got a copy of the paper that my cousin had . . . Which her kids throwed away.

1427
01:08:00.386 --> 01:08:02.242
Just don’t throw history away.

1428
01:08:02.242 --> 01:08:11.523
That they grandma, which Grandma Pinky’s sister, her mother sold her sister to Jim Lee for a sack of rice, a mule, and some sugar.

1429
01:08:11.523 --> 01:08:13.750
And it was all on paper.

1430
01:08:13.750 --> 01:08:19.689
And she wind up marrying . . . his son was Jim Lee Junior who was a white man,

1431
01:08:19.689 --> 01:08:24.515
and so that’s how the Lees coming into . . . with us, with the family.

1432
01:08:24.515 --> 01:08:25.258
It’s amazing.

1433
01:08:25.258 --> 01:08:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1434
01:08:26.000 --> 01:08:26.613
<v HUNTSBERRY>It’s amazing.

1435
01:08:26.613 --> 01:08:31.827
And I’m . . . like I told Joe I saw the paper myself because she showed it to me.

1436
01:08:31.827 --> 01:08:35.813
Because when I was sitting on the porch, she said look at this.

1437
01:08:35.813 --> 01:08:38.573
And I said, “[Bootsie?] you need to keep that.”

1438
01:08:38.573 --> 01:08:40.720
And she had it in a Bible.

1439
01:08:40.720 --> 01:08:44.707
Some kids throwed all her stuff . . . I just couldn’t believe they did that.

1440
01:08:44.707 --> 01:08:47.467
That’s . . . you just don’t throw stuff away like that.

1441
01:08:47.467 --> 01:08:49.000
And that’s how they did.

1442
01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:51.760
That’s how they did back then in them days.

1443
01:08:51.760 --> 01:08:52.987
That’s how they . . . They

1444
01:08:52.987 --> 01:08:53.600
<c.p>did that.

1445
01:08:53.600 --> 01:08:56.360
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was the . . . Was there a doctor in Mossville?

1446
01:08:56.360 --> 01:08:57.280
<v HUNTSBERRY>I don’t remember.

1447
01:08:57.280 --> 01:09:02.187
I remember old man . . . I can’t call that old man, but I can ask my brother.

1448
01:09:02.187 --> 01:09:07.707
I don’t . . . They had an old man who used to treat people for worms and stuff like that.

1449
01:09:07.707 --> 01:09:12.000
He would come on a bicycle and I used to take off running.

1450
01:09:12.000 --> 01:09:13.100
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he scary?

1451
01:09:13.100 --> 01:09:14.200
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yes he was.

1452
01:09:14.200 --> 01:09:16.033
Let me ask my brother.

1453
01:09:16.033 --> 01:09:17.867
Can I ask my brother?

1454
01:09:17.867 --> 01:09:20.067
Let me see if he remember.

1455
01:09:20.067 --> 01:09:20.433
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure.

1456
01:09:20.433 --> 01:09:22.267
Want me to pause it?

1457
01:09:22.267 --> 01:09:23.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah.

1458
01:09:23.000 --> 01:09:26.448
<v ARSENEAULT>You can pause it real quick Kyle? [Break in tape].

1459
01:09:26.448 --> 01:09:28.517
So it was “mamou tea man” . . .

1460
01:09:28.517 --> 01:09:36.448
<v HUNTSBERRY>“Mamou tea man” they . . . he said they called him “Mamou tea man” but he’s going to find out his real name right quick,

1461
01:09:36.448 --> 01:09:36.793
but . . .

1462
01:09:36.793 --> 01:09:37.138
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1463
01:09:37.138 --> 01:09:39.207
Did he make the mamou tea?

1464
01:09:39.207 --> 01:09:39.897
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, yeah.

1465
01:09:39.897 --> 01:09:43.000
We used to have mamou tea . . . Oh, Lord.

1466
01:09:43.000 --> 01:09:44.136
<v ARSENEAULT>What is mamou tea?

1467
01:09:44.136 --> 01:09:48.114
<v HUNTSBERRY>Mamou . . . it came from an herb, a tree . . . Momma used to find the limbs.

1468
01:09:48.114 --> 01:09:52.659
I don’t know, but she said you can smell it when you go in the woods,

1469
01:09:52.659 --> 01:09:57.205
but we never could find it, but she knew where to go to find it at.

1470
01:09:57.205 --> 01:10:04.875
And they would take it . . . it’s like a . . . she’ll break it off a . . . it’s like off a tree and she’ll break it and then she’ll boil it.

1471
01:10:04.875 --> 01:10:06.864
And it’d be that pretty pinkish color.

1472
01:10:06.864 --> 01:10:11.409
And talk about . . . it took all the cold you had in you, it’ll take it out.

1473
01:10:11.409 --> 01:10:11.977
You’ll sweat.

1474
01:10:11.977 --> 01:10:14.250
They had mamou tea and we drunk that.

1475
01:10:14.250 --> 01:10:15.102
We drunk that.

1476
01:10:15.102 --> 01:10:16.239
They gave you that.

1477
01:10:16.239 --> 01:10:17.659
Sapphire . . . they had sapphire tea.

1478
01:10:17.659 --> 01:10:18.511
We had that.

1479
01:10:18.511 --> 01:10:20.500
And they knew what it look like.

1480
01:10:20.500 --> 01:10:21.068
We didn’t.

1481
01:10:21.068 --> 01:10:23.909
I thought it was just like an old tree, me.

1482
01:10:23.909 --> 01:10:24.761
I didn’t know.

1483
01:10:24.761 --> 01:10:27.602
Because they knew what to . . . how to go find that.

1484
01:10:27.602 --> 01:10:28.739
And they did that.

1485
01:10:28.739 --> 01:10:30.727
<v ARSENEAULT>What would y’all do for like colds?

1486
01:10:30.727 --> 01:10:31.864
<v HUNTSBERRY>That’s what we did.

1487
01:10:31.864 --> 01:10:33.000
The Mamou tea.

1488
01:10:33.000 --> 01:10:34.217
<v ARSENEAULT>That was mamou tea?

1489
01:10:34.217 --> 01:10:34.521
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Agrees].

1490
01:10:34.521 --> 01:10:38.779
The teas and stuff, but that was it, but it wasn’t often that we

1491
01:10:38.779 --> 01:10:39.388
<c.p>catch colds.

1492
01:10:39.388 --> 01:10:40.604
It wasn’t that often.

1493
01:10:40.604 --> 01:10:46.992
And then that old man he would come and [. . .?] now you say, “I ain’t got no worm where the worm is?

1494
01:10:46.992 --> 01:10:49.729
I don’t know nothing about no worms or whatever.”

1495
01:10:49.729 --> 01:10:50.946
I’ll take off running.

1496
01:10:50.946 --> 01:10:52.467
Oh, he was scary looking.

1497
01:10:52.467 --> 01:10:53.988
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did he come from?

1498
01:10:53.988 --> 01:10:56.117
<v HUNTSBERRY>I don’t know where he stayed at,

1499
01:10:56.117 --> 01:11:07.067
but he would come on an old bicycle with a [tray?] in the front of a [cart?] and he’d have all that old stuff in bottles and here he come pumping down the road treating people.

1500
01:11:07.067 --> 01:11:07.979
Oh, no. [Disagrees].

1501
01:11:07.979 --> 01:11:09.500
He looked scary to me.

1502
01:11:09.500 --> 01:11:10.717
I took off running.

1503
01:11:10.717 --> 01:11:13.454
I think I was probably about five or six.

1504
01:11:13.454 --> 01:11:14.975
Honey I take off running.

1505
01:11:14.975 --> 01:11:19.842
They used to look for me I’d be in the woods, behind the house was woods.

1506
01:11:19.842 --> 01:11:22.579
I’d take off running go hide in the woods.

1507
01:11:22.579 --> 01:11:25.621
Oh, no. We’ll climb get on top of the house.

1508
01:11:25.621 --> 01:11:30.488
I climb . . . Girl they be looking for me I’ll be sitting on top of the house.

1509
01:11:30.488 --> 01:11:33.529
Oh, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, [disagrees].

1510
01:11:33.529 --> 01:11:34.746
No. He was scary.

1511
01:11:34.746 --> 01:11:35.962
He was really scary.

1512
01:11:35.962 --> 01:11:36.875
I don’t know.

1513
01:11:36.875 --> 01:11:42.046
And he would go to everybody’s house, so they knew him because he would go and treat.

1514
01:11:42.046 --> 01:11:43.262
Yeah, I don’t know.

1515
01:11:43.262 --> 01:11:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any other treaters in the area?

1516
01:11:46.000 --> 01:11:49.519
<v HUNTSBERRY>That’s the only one I would know at that time.

1517
01:11:49.519 --> 01:11:50.574
I don’t know.

1518
01:11:50.574 --> 01:11:54.796
And then after that we start going to Doctor Smith in Sulphur.

1519
01:11:54.796 --> 01:12:01.130
Charles Smith where we’ll go to his office and we . . . That became our doctor then, our family doctor.

1520
01:12:01.130 --> 01:12:02.185
Was . . . Thank God.

1521
01:12:02.185 --> 01:12:03.241
Lord have mercy.

1522
01:12:03.241 --> 01:12:07.111
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any other like, home remedies for cuts or bruises?

1523
01:12:07.111 --> 01:12:12.389
<v HUNTSBERRY>Home remedies . . . They had a lot of home remedies Momma and them used to do.

1524
01:12:12.389 --> 01:12:15.204
Especially for spider, I mean not spider, wasp.

1525
01:12:15.204 --> 01:12:25.056
They used to take like the tobacco and wet it and put it on there and wrap your arm and it draw the stinger out with the tobacco.

1526
01:12:25.056 --> 01:12:26.815
They used to do that.

1527
01:12:26.815 --> 01:12:32.444
But she used to make some kind of . . . we used to get like boils or something,

1528
01:12:32.444 --> 01:12:35.611
she would make something out of like, gumbo file.

1529
01:12:35.611 --> 01:12:42.296
She would take and make it like a paste and put on that and then it draws the head . . .

1530
01:12:42.296 --> 01:12:43.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Sassafras.

1531
01:12:43.000 --> 01:12:44.687
<v HUNTSBERRY>And it’ll draw the head and stuff.

1532
01:12:44.687 --> 01:12:46.133
They would do stuff like that.

1533
01:12:46.133 --> 01:12:46.614
Home remedies.

1534
01:12:46.614 --> 01:12:48.301
They had a whole lot of stuff.

1535
01:12:48.301 --> 01:12:54.325
She used to have bad headaches that . . . Warm salt in a pot and put it in a handkerchief and set on top of her head.

1536
01:12:54.325 --> 01:12:56.494
And she said used to her bring her down.

1537
01:12:56.494 --> 01:13:00.831
I said I’m going to try that one day if I ever . . . To see if that really work.

1538
01:13:00.831 --> 01:13:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She just put salt water on her head?

1539
01:13:03.000 --> 01:13:07.370
<v HUNTSBERRY>Just . . . She would put the salt in a pot then heat the salt.

1540
01:13:07.370 --> 01:13:14.429
Heat it real . . . And pour it in a handkerchief and tie it, and set the handkerchief on top of her head.

1541
01:13:14.429 --> 01:13:18.798
And she said it would help bring that . . . Make her headache go away.

1542
01:13:18.798 --> 01:13:21.487
And I said I’m going to try that.

1543
01:13:21.487 --> 01:13:28.210
I’m not one to head headaches, but I used to laugh I said I’m going to try that one day.

1544
01:13:28.210 --> 01:13:29.891
I’m still yet to try.

1545
01:13:29.891 --> 01:13:33.588
But they used to do that and . . . basically that’s . . . basically we . . .

1546
01:13:33.588 --> 01:13:35.269
<v ARSENEAULT>Anything special for a fever?

1547
01:13:35.269 --> 01:13:39.639
<v HUNTSBERRY>No. like I said we had that mamou tea and stuff like that.

1548
01:13:39.639 --> 01:13:40.647
That was it.

1549
01:13:40.647 --> 01:13:41.992
No. No we didn’t . . .

1550
01:13:41.992 --> 01:13:43.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Broken bones?

1551
01:13:43.000 --> 01:13:49.437
<v HUNTSBERRY>No. No. Now, my sister and my brother was playing and [toys and?] Indians she broke her jaw,

1552
01:13:49.437 --> 01:13:53.371
but she went to the emergency . . . To the hospital for that.

1553
01:13:53.371 --> 01:13:55.517
But no, I don’t know. [Disagrees].

1554
01:13:55.517 --> 01:13:59.808
I don’t remember . . . No, ain’t nobody had no broken bones back then.

1555
01:13:59.808 --> 01:14:02.669
She the only one with the broken jaw.

1556
01:14:02.669 --> 01:14:03.026
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1557
01:14:03.026 --> 01:14:13.397
<v HUNTSBERRY>She fell . . . He dropped off top of the house and she fell on the creek by them big stumps and broke her jaw in five places broke her jaw.

1558
01:14:13.397 --> 01:14:14.470
I remember that.

1559
01:14:14.470 --> 01:14:19.834
They wire her mouth up so she had to go to the hospital for that.

1560
01:14:19.834 --> 01:14:25.556
But other than that, we didn’t . . . we had some cuts but you put . . . fat on it.

1561
01:14:25.556 --> 01:14:27.344
That hog fat put there.

1562
01:14:27.344 --> 01:14:33.066
She’ll put that on there and stuff like that but other than that, that was it.

1563
01:14:33.066 --> 01:14:34.139
That was it.

1564
01:14:34.139 --> 01:14:35.212
Or they call . . .

1565
01:14:35.212 --> 01:14:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Hog fat for cuts?

1566
01:14:37.000 --> 01:14:37.925
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, yeah.

1567
01:14:37.925 --> 01:14:42.090
Or they’ll have that blue-in . . . it called blue-in or

1568
01:14:42.090 --> 01:14:42.552
<c.p>something.

1569
01:14:42.552 --> 01:14:52.269
Or something else they would call . . . it was red and they put on your cuts and stuff and bandage it up,

1570
01:14:52.269 --> 01:14:54.119
but that was it.

1571
01:14:54.119 --> 01:14:57.821
We didn’t have too much, us, not us.

1572
01:14:57.821 --> 01:15:00.597
Not that I can remember, so.

1573
01:15:00.597 --> 01:15:02.910
So but that was it.

1574
01:15:02.910 --> 01:15:04.299
<v ARSENEAULT>What about church?

1575
01:15:04.299 --> 01:15:07.075
<v HUNTSBERRY>Like I said church was at . . .

1576
01:15:07.075 --> 01:15:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Philadelphia?

1577
01:15:08.000 --> 01:15:10.151
<v HUNTSBERRY>Philadelphia of God and Christ.

1578
01:15:10.151 --> 01:15:14.453
It was sanctified church and honey, they had some church.

1579
01:15:14.453 --> 01:15:17.035
<v ARSENEAULT>How many churches were in Mossville?

1580
01:15:17.035 --> 01:15:22.198
<v HUNTSBERRY>One, two, three, four . . . I remember about five of them for sure.

1581
01:15:22.198 --> 01:15:23.919
It was . . . Oh, Lord.

1582
01:15:23.919 --> 01:15:26.070
What was Mr. Rigmaiden’s church?

1583
01:15:26.070 --> 01:15:34.244
They had the Morning Star, Philadelphia of God and Christ, Mount Zion . . . Golly I can’t think of that church.

1584
01:15:34.244 --> 01:15:43.709
It used to be on the corner on Prater Road and Mr. Rigmaiden was the . . . the other Rigmaiden, it was another Rigmaiden.

1585
01:15:43.709 --> 01:15:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Christ Holiness?

1586
01:15:45.000 --> 01:15:45.655
<v HUNTSBERRY>Probably so.

1587
01:15:45.655 --> 01:15:45.983
Probably.

1588
01:15:45.983 --> 01:15:48.603
I’m going to have to ask my brother.

1589
01:15:48.603 --> 01:15:52.862
<v ARSENEAULT>I have a hard time keeping track because there’s a lot of them.

1590
01:15:52.862 --> 01:15:53.845
<v HUNTSBERRY>Because it’s a . . .

1591
01:15:53.845 --> 01:15:54.828
<v ARSENEAULT>The Mount Zion . . .

1592
01:15:54.828 --> 01:15:55.155
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah.

1593
01:15:55.155 --> 01:15:59.086
It was on the corner of Old Spanish Trail and Prater Road.

1594
01:15:59.086 --> 01:16:01.379
I don’t remember because I never did . . .

1595
01:16:01.379 --> 01:16:03.345
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a Methodist Church maybe?

1596
01:16:03.345 --> 01:16:04.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Huh?

1597
01:16:04.000 --> 01:16:05.525
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a Methodist?

1598
01:16:05.525 --> 01:16:06.287
Or Baptist?

1599
01:16:06.287 --> 01:16:09.718
<v HUNTSBERRY>I don’t know if it was Methodist or Baptist.

1600
01:16:09.718 --> 01:16:11.624
I never did go there.

1601
01:16:11.624 --> 01:16:23.822
So like I said only church I went to was Philadelphia God and Christ with my grandma and then we was Catholic at that time so we was at Saint John’s Bosco.

1602
01:16:23.822 --> 01:16:31.827
But that’s what . . . basically the two churches that I went to mostly before I got older and went to Mount Zion.

1603
01:16:31.827 --> 01:16:36.020
I went to Mount Zion after that, but other than that,

1604
01:16:36.020 --> 01:16:42.500
the two churches I attend was Philadelphia and then we went to Saint John Bosco in Westlake.

1605
01:16:42.500 --> 01:16:46.693
<v ARSENEAULT>Who just . . . How did people decide what church to go to?

1606
01:16:46.693 --> 01:16:54.698
<v HUNTSBERRY>Well we was really . . . Really I just went because of grandma, because we was with grandma, but we was raised Catholic.

1607
01:16:54.698 --> 01:16:56.223
We was raised Catholic.

1608
01:16:56.223 --> 01:17:04.228
And back then you didn’t . . . I couldn’t even understand the word the priest was saying because he was talking in Latin.

1609
01:17:04.228 --> 01:17:06.515
I went “What was he saying?”

1610
01:17:06.515 --> 01:17:08.040
We didn’t understand that.

1611
01:17:08.040 --> 01:17:12.614
So and then finally as I got older then I went Baptist.

1612
01:17:12.614 --> 01:17:16.045
And now they talk where you can understand them.

1613
01:17:16.045 --> 01:17:18.332
Oh, well I done moved on.

1614
01:17:18.332 --> 01:17:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What made you decide to switch?

1615
01:17:21.000 --> 01:17:25.374
<v HUNTSBERRY>My mom moved and when she moved, we just moved with mom.

1616
01:17:25.374 --> 01:17:27.196
We didn’t ask no questions.

1617
01:17:27.196 --> 01:17:29.748
Wherever she went, that’s where we went.

1618
01:17:29.748 --> 01:17:35.944
And that was how it was, so she wound up going Baptist and that’s what we did.

1619
01:17:35.944 --> 01:17:36.673
Went Baptist.

1620
01:17:36.673 --> 01:17:37.402
Yeah, so.

1621
01:17:37.402 --> 01:17:38.495
I told Momma.

1622
01:17:38.495 --> 01:17:44.692
I said, “I can’t understand a word that man . . .” And I had catechism now, did my communion.

1623
01:17:44.692 --> 01:17:46.514
I did all of that.

1624
01:17:46.514 --> 01:17:48.336
I did it all, so.

1625
01:17:48.336 --> 01:17:50.523
So but, I didn’t understand them.

1626
01:17:50.523 --> 01:17:51.981
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean first communion?

1627
01:17:51.981 --> 01:17:53.439
You did first communion?

1628
01:17:53.439 --> 01:17:54.533
<v HUNTSBERRY>All of it.

1629
01:17:54.533 --> 01:17:56.355
I had it all done.

1630
01:17:56.355 --> 01:17:57.449
Did it all.

1631
01:17:57.449 --> 01:17:58.178
Did it . . .

1632
01:17:58.178 --> 01:18:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it in Latin?

1633
01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:01.505
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah it was in Latin.

1634
01:18:01.505 --> 01:18:07.226
Yeah when I was coming up that’s why I used to tell my momma, “What is that man saying?”

1635
01:18:07.226 --> 01:18:09.032
Everybody stand up, you stand up.

1636
01:18:09.032 --> 01:18:10.839
<c.p>Everybody sit down, you sit down.

1637
01:18:10.839 --> 01:18:11.742
We didn’t know.

1638
01:18:11.742 --> 01:18:13.247
We . . . I couldn’t understand him.

1639
01:18:13.247 --> 01:18:17.462
I couldn’t understand him, so but when she went to Baptist I said, yeah.

1640
01:18:17.462 --> 01:18:18.366
Here we go.

1641
01:18:18.366 --> 01:18:24.688
And that’s when we went to Baptist and that’s when I got into the junior choir and stuff like that, so.

1642
01:18:24.688 --> 01:18:28.000
But now from that day on I still stayed Baptist.

1643
01:18:28.000 --> 01:18:30.311
<v ARSENEAULT>So y’all started going to Mount Zion?

1644
01:18:30.311 --> 01:18:34.932
<v HUNTSBERRY>We . . . yeah we . . . No, Momma went to Mount Calvary out here at Lake Charles.

1645
01:18:34.932 --> 01:18:42.854
We came . . . We drove all the way over here to Lake Charles to go to Mount Calvary and that’s where we started being Baptist.

1646
01:18:42.854 --> 01:18:46.485
And then we left Mount Calvary and went to Mount Zion.

1647
01:18:46.485 --> 01:18:51.107
And then after my brother, who I just talked to, he became a minister.

1648
01:18:51.107 --> 01:18:54.408
Then I left Mount Zion and went to his church.

1649
01:18:54.408 --> 01:18:56.058
I’m with my brother now.

1650
01:18:56.058 --> 01:18:56.718
Yeah, so.

1651
01:18:56.718 --> 01:18:58.369
That’s where I’m at now.

1652
01:18:58.369 --> 01:19:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was the pastor at Mount Zion at the time?

1653
01:19:02.000 --> 01:19:07.825
<v HUNTSBERRY>At the time it was . . . When I went it was my cousin.

1654
01:19:07.825 --> 01:19:09.767
It was Arthur [Achan?].

1655
01:19:09.767 --> 01:19:16.078
When I . . . When we joined at Mount Zion he was the pastor then.

1656
01:19:16.078 --> 01:19:23.845
And then like I said . . . then when after . . . and even my brother . . . we was all there.

1657
01:19:23.845 --> 01:19:31.612
We was all at Mount Zion and when he became a pastor, then we all left.

1658
01:19:31.612 --> 01:19:38.408
We all left and went to his church, so we went to True Light.

1659
01:19:38.408 --> 01:19:39.864
<v ARSENEAULT>I got you.

1660
01:19:39.864 --> 01:19:45.689
<v HUNTSBERRY>He’s a pastor at True Light Baptist church here in Lake Charles.

1661
01:19:45.689 --> 01:19:46.175
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1662
01:19:46.175 --> 01:19:52.000
So what is your earliest childhood memory when you think back?

1663
01:19:52.000 --> 01:19:57.435
<v HUNTSBERRY>Basically like I said the earliest part is us just having fun.

1664
01:19:57.435 --> 01:20:08.306
I think the part that I really liked when Halloween come and everybody wore . . . Dress up in your mother’s clothes or something like that,

1665
01:20:08.306 --> 01:20:18.724
or Mrs. Eula be mad at her son because he’ll take her sheet and run down the road like he was a ghost.

1666
01:20:18.724 --> 01:20:20.535
And we had fun.

1667
01:20:20.535 --> 01:20:21.894
We had fun.

1668
01:20:21.894 --> 01:20:26.876
And we’ll put bags . . . we’ll draw our costumes out of bags.

1669
01:20:26.876 --> 01:20:28.688
And we did that.

1670
01:20:28.688 --> 01:20:30.047
Everybody did that.

1671
01:20:30.047 --> 01:20:32.312
We just all had fun.

1672
01:20:32.312 --> 01:20:40.012
And I think that was the biggest fun for the whole community at that time for Halloween.

1673
01:20:40.012 --> 01:20:46.353
And basically that’s it besides that and playing softball with my cousins and them.

1674
01:20:46.353 --> 01:20:48.165
That was basically it.

1675
01:20:48.165 --> 01:20:50.882
Besides that we was working, honey.

1676
01:20:50.882 --> 01:20:51.788
Home working.

1677
01:20:51.788 --> 01:20:54.959
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any ghost stories in Mossville?

1678
01:20:54.959 --> 01:20:55.865
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, yeah.

1679
01:20:55.865 --> 01:20:57.224
The sycamore tree.

1680
01:20:57.224 --> 01:21:03.112
They used to talk about the sycamore tree on . . . [to grandson] Get him.

1681
01:21:03.112 --> 01:21:04.018
Get him.

1682
01:21:04.018 --> 01:21:05.376
<v ARSENEAULT>He got scared.

1683
01:21:05.376 --> 01:21:09.000
He thought he was going to fall.

1684
01:21:09.000 --> 01:21:09.756
<v HUNTSBERRY>You okay?

1685
01:21:09.756 --> 01:21:10.891
The sycamore tree.

1686
01:21:10.891 --> 01:21:15.808
They would talk about the sycamore tree running people on Houston River Road.

1687
01:21:15.808 --> 01:21:24.506
That’s what they used talk about all the time, but I never would go down there to find out, I guarantee you that.

1688
01:21:24.506 --> 01:21:26.019
But that’s what they

1689
01:21:26.019 --> 01:21:29.045
<c.p>would always talk about is the sycamore tree.

1690
01:21:29.045 --> 01:21:30.558
<v ARSENEAULT>What would they say?

1691
01:21:30.558 --> 01:21:35.853
<v HUNTSBERRY>How it was . . . talk about somebody was chasing them or something. [. . .?] I don’t know.

1692
01:21:35.853 --> 01:21:37.744
I never went down there.

1693
01:21:37.744 --> 01:21:41.147
I never . . . I wasn’t going down there see it.

1694
01:21:41.147 --> 01:21:42.282
I wasn’t going.

1695
01:21:42.282 --> 01:21:44.929
They really talked about the sycamore tree.

1696
01:21:44.929 --> 01:21:46.064
I don’t know.

1697
01:21:46.064 --> 01:21:47.577
But that’s basically it.

1698
01:21:47.577 --> 01:21:54.006
Like I said I wasn’t too much of a person that goes around a lot of people.

1699
01:21:54.006 --> 01:21:54.763
I wasn’t.

1700
01:21:54.763 --> 01:22:00.814
My brothers and them . . . they was older than me so they branched out, but I didn’t.

1701
01:22:00.814 --> 01:22:05.353
But yeah they talked about the sycamore tree. [Child crying] Be quiet.

1702
01:22:05.353 --> 01:22:06.109
Be quiet.

1703
01:22:06.109 --> 01:22:06.865
Be quiet.

1704
01:22:06.865 --> 01:22:08.000
Yeah, so.

1705
01:22:08.000 --> 01:22:10.435
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have any childhood role models?

1706
01:22:10.435 --> 01:22:11.478
Anything like that?

1707
01:22:11.478 --> 01:22:12.174
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, [disagrees].

1708
01:22:12.174 --> 01:22:12.522
No.

1709
01:22:12.522 --> 01:22:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Nobody you wanted to be when you grew up?

1710
01:22:16.000 --> 01:22:18.188
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, no. [Disagrees].

1711
01:22:18.188 --> 01:22:18.917
Stop.

1712
01:22:18.917 --> 01:22:19.646
No.

1713
01:22:19.646 --> 01:22:22.562
<v ARSENEAULT>What about local musicians?

1714
01:22:22.562 --> 01:22:26.208
Were there any local musicians?

1715
01:22:26.208 --> 01:22:26.938
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Disagrees].

1716
01:22:26.938 --> 01:22:27.667
No.

1717
01:22:27.667 --> 01:22:31.312
<v ARSENEAULT>Any Zydeco musicians or anything?

1718
01:22:31.312 --> 01:22:32.042
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Disagrees].

1719
01:22:32.042 --> 01:22:36.417
No, we didn’t . . . No, I didn’t.

1720
01:22:36.417 --> 01:22:44.438
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ve heard the name Garfield [Verdine?] . . . Does that ring any bells?

1721
01:22:44.438 --> 01:22:45.167
<v HUNTSBERRY>Well . . .

1722
01:22:45.167 --> 01:22:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That might have been a while ago.

1723
01:22:51.000 --> 01:22:51.914
<v HUNTSBERRY>Probably so.

1724
01:22:51.914 --> 01:22:57.857
Probably way before . . . like I say my brothers and them probably knew him.

1725
01:22:57.857 --> 01:22:58.314
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1726
01:22:58.314 --> 01:23:04.257
<v HUNTSBERRY>But Verdine was like I said . . . the Verdines was like . . . My great grandmother,

1727
01:23:04.257 --> 01:23:10.200
she was a Verdine before she married my uncle, my grandfather I mean.

1728
01:23:10.200 --> 01:23:18.886
No, see we didn’t do . . . My mom and them did all that stuff, but she never talked about it.

1729
01:23:18.886 --> 01:23:20.714
They were . . . Stop, Dana.

1730
01:23:20.714 --> 01:23:26.657
She would . . . they had a picture of her and my daddy and them.

1731
01:23:26.657 --> 01:23:35.343
They’d be at the Dog Hill Club and stuff like that, but they wouldn’t . . . talk about what they did.

1732
01:23:35.343 --> 01:23:36.257
No. no.

1733
01:23:36.257 --> 01:23:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They played instruments, your grandparents?

1734
01:23:39.000 --> 01:23:42.418
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, my uncle on my . . . My momma’s brother did.

1735
01:23:42.418 --> 01:23:45.456
He had a . . . Matter of fact he did.

1736
01:23:45.456 --> 01:23:47.354
He had a Zydeco band.

1737
01:23:47.354 --> 01:23:48.494
He sure did.

1738
01:23:48.494 --> 01:23:53.810
Achan . . . He would play in a Zydeco band, but I don’t remember the name.

1739
01:23:53.810 --> 01:23:56.848
And that was years, years, years, years ago.

1740
01:23:56.848 --> 01:23:58.367
He had a [accordion?].

1741
01:23:58.367 --> 01:24:07.101
And matter of fact, when he died I think my Uncle Arthur had got that [accordion?] that he would play all the time,

1742
01:24:07.101 --> 01:24:09.759
but . . . it was an old one, too.

1743
01:24:09.759 --> 01:24:11.278
A very old one.

1744
01:24:11.278 --> 01:24:11.658
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1745
01:24:11.658 --> 01:24:20.392
<v HUNTSBERRY>But then you could give it to him, he had Alzheimer’s, and you put it in his hand he’ll start playing and singing,

1746
01:24:20.392 --> 01:24:26.468
but he couldn’t remember our names, nobody’s name, but he could play that thing and sing.

1747
01:24:26.468 --> 01:24:28.367
<v ARSENEAULT>He remember how to play.

1748
01:24:28.367 --> 01:24:30.266
<v HUNTSBERRY>He remember how to play.

1749
01:24:30.266 --> 01:24:32.165
Before he died he remember.

1750
01:24:32.165 --> 01:24:37.101
It was amazing. [To grandson] Stop, Dana, before you knock something down, baby.

1751
01:24:37.101 --> 01:24:37.481
Stop.

1752
01:24:37.481 --> 01:24:39.000
Dana stop, baby!

1753
01:24:39.000 --> 01:24:42.256
<v ARSENEAULT>So growing up, who made like the decisions in your family?

1754
01:24:42.256 --> 01:24:43.735
<v HUNTSBERRY>My mom and dad did.

1755
01:24:43.735 --> 01:24:45.215
My mom . . . Basically my mom.

1756
01:24:45.215 --> 01:24:46.695
My dad was a workman.

1757
01:24:46.695 --> 01:24:50.543
He’ll . . . would go to work . . . He’ll leave in the morning go to work,

1758
01:24:50.543 --> 01:24:53.798
he’ll come back in the evening, and go in his room.

1759
01:24:53.798 --> 01:24:55.278
He’ll go in his room.

1760
01:24:55.278 --> 01:24:57.646
He’ll eat and then he’ll go to bed.

1761
01:24:57.646 --> 01:25:00.901
He’ll sleep until the next morning until he go to work.

1762
01:25:00.901 --> 01:25:03.269
And unless . . . Like I said on weekends he’ll

1763
01:25:03.269 --> 01:25:07.413
<c.p>take my momma to the store to get a few little things or whatever,

1764
01:25:07.413 --> 01:25:11.260
about cutting grass and stuff, but most of the time it was Momma.

1765
01:25:11.260 --> 01:25:12.148
It was Momma.

1766
01:25:12.148 --> 01:25:18.067
Momma handled everything because like I said he’ll go to work, and he’ll come back, and he’ll go to bed.

1767
01:25:18.067 --> 01:25:18.659
It’d be . . .

1768
01:25:18.659 --> 01:25:20.139
<v ARSENEAULT>He worked at the stevedore . . . ?

1769
01:25:20.139 --> 01:25:21.027
<v HUNTSBERRY>Lake Charles Stevedore.

1770
01:25:21.027 --> 01:25:27.242
He work at the docks and he’ll come in at five o’clock and . . . [phone ringing] He’ll come in at five o’clock.

1771
01:25:27.242 --> 01:25:28.426
That’s my other brother.

1772
01:25:28.426 --> 01:25:30.202
He going to call me back.

1773
01:25:30.202 --> 01:25:36.121
He’ll work at Lake Charles Stevedore, and when he come in about five, he’ll go straight on in the room.

1774
01:25:36.121 --> 01:25:39.377
Do what he have to do and he’ll go to bed.

1775
01:25:39.377 --> 01:25:45.000
After he’d gone to sleep you don’t see him until the next morning he going out the door.

1776
01:25:45.000 --> 01:25:48.224
<v ARSENEAULT>What did he do at the docks?

1777
01:25:48.224 --> 01:25:51.447
<v HUNTSBERRY>He was a laborer at the time,

1778
01:25:51.447 --> 01:26:01.579
and then as the years went on he would drive the little tractors and stuff like that, but that’s what they did.

1779
01:26:01.579 --> 01:26:09.408
They did they . . . They did unload the cargos for the rice and flours and stuff like that.

1780
01:26:09.408 --> 01:26:11.711
They did all of that.

