<v HUNTSBERRY>You just . . . you really just chew it because you chewing the sweetness out of it and throw the stuff out.

2004
01:37:30.847 --> 01:37:31.573
That’s all.

2005
01:37:31.573 --> 01:37:35.927
Basically what you doing is just chewing the sweetness and [you gone?].

2006
01:37:35.927 --> 01:37:37.016
It was good.

2007
01:37:37.016 --> 01:37:39.194
I’d love, Lord have mercy, sugarcane.

2008
01:37:39.194 --> 01:37:41.371
We ate a lot of them.

2009
01:37:41.371 --> 01:37:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they send it to get processed or anything?

2010
01:37:45.000 --> 01:37:45.328
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Disagrees].

2011
01:37:45.328 --> 01:37:47.625
Grandma used to do her own stuff.

2012
01:37:47.625 --> 01:37:57.469
She . . . basically like I said it was like . . . they would have it under the porch in a container and you just get it and peel it and keep it going.

2013
01:37:57.469 --> 01:37:59.766
No, she didn’t process it or nothing.

2014
01:37:59.766 --> 01:38:03.375
Everybody in the community had some sugarcane I guarantee you that.

2015
01:38:03.375 --> 01:38:04.359
Everybody had some.

2016
01:38:04.359 --> 01:38:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They all grew it?

2017
01:38:06.000 --> 01:38:10.964
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, because . . . Down the street like I said they took care of each other.

2018
01:38:10.964 --> 01:38:14.155
They’ll just go to the house and get a . . .

2019
01:38:14.155 --> 01:38:15.218
<v ARSENEAULT>Just share it.

2020
01:38:15.218 --> 01:38:21.245
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah and get a . . . and if this one got some purple hulled [?] peas they’ll share that too.

2021
01:38:21.245 --> 01:38:22.664
That’s how they did.

2022
01:38:22.664 --> 01:38:24.436
That’s how they did, honey.

2023
01:38:24.436 --> 01:38:29.400
But yeah them . . . we used to go in that garden and cut them sugarcane.

2024
01:38:29.400 --> 01:38:31.527
Lord, we used to have machetes.

2025
01:38:31.527 --> 01:38:41.809
Them sharp knives and grandpa used to . . . Matter of fact I got four machetes from him that they used to cut they grass and cut sugarcane and stuff with.

2026
01:38:41.809 --> 01:38:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They would cut the grass with it too?

2027
01:38:45.000 --> 01:38:47.800
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, yeah they’ll cut the grass with machetes.

2028
01:38:47.800 --> 01:38:48.500
Oh yeah.

2029
01:38:48.500 --> 01:38:49.200
Oh yeah.

2030
01:38:49.200 --> 01:39:00.050
Like the big machetes they’ll sit out here . . . Sit out under the porch and sharpen that thing and he’ll just go and just . . . and I mean that grass will be flying.

2031
01:39:00.050 --> 01:39:02.150
That’s how they cut the grass.

2032
01:39:02.150 --> 01:39:04.250
<c.p>That’s how they cut the grass.

2033
01:39:04.250 --> 01:39:05.300
With the machetes.

2034
01:39:05.300 --> 01:39:07.050
<v ARSENEAULT>How would they sharpen it?

2035
01:39:07.050 --> 01:39:14.050
<v HUNTSBERRY>They had a stone . . . Like a stone or something he used to have because they didn’t have files back then.

2036
01:39:14.050 --> 01:39:15.450
They had a stone.

2037
01:39:15.450 --> 01:39:19.650
And he would sit under that [car?] porch and sharpen that thing.

2038
01:39:19.650 --> 01:39:21.750
Or either they would . . . the step.

2039
01:39:21.750 --> 01:39:26.300
They use the side of the step and they’ll sharpen it on that.

2040
01:39:26.300 --> 01:39:27.700
So they did that.

2041
01:39:27.700 --> 01:39:32.250
That thing used to be so sharp the grass would just be flying.

2042
01:39:32.250 --> 01:39:34.000
So they did that.

2043
01:39:34.000 --> 01:39:35.075
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the house?

2044
01:39:35.075 --> 01:39:37.493
Do you know who built your house in Mossville?

2045
01:39:37.493 --> 01:39:43.940
<v HUNTSBERRY>No the house was for my grandfather gave it to my mom and they house . . . I think my brother said they built they house,

2046
01:39:43.940 --> 01:39:45.552
but it was a beautiful house.

2047
01:39:45.552 --> 01:39:46.896
That thing was so gorgeous.

2048
01:39:46.896 --> 01:39:48.239
<v ARSENEAULT>What did it look like?

2049
01:39:48.239 --> 01:39:49.045
<v HUNTSBERRY>It was big.

2050
01:39:49.045 --> 01:39:49.851
It was huge.

2051
01:39:49.851 --> 01:39:50.388
It had . . .

2052
01:39:50.388 --> 01:39:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it still in Mossville?

2053
01:39:52.000 --> 01:39:57.231
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, my daddy had to tear it down, no. It had one, two, three,

2054
01:39:57.231 --> 01:40:00.593
four . . . maybe five, six bedrooms, two bath . . . three baths.

2055
01:40:00.593 --> 01:40:03.209
A big old den, a big kitchen.

2056
01:40:03.209 --> 01:40:04.703
That kitchen was huge.

2057
01:40:04.703 --> 01:40:07.319
A living room . . . Oh, I remember that.

2058
01:40:07.319 --> 01:40:12.176
Had to walk through the glass doors to go into the living room.

2059
01:40:12.176 --> 01:40:19.648
That floor used to shine so bad I said you better not wear a dress you’ll see everything under there.

2060
01:40:19.648 --> 01:40:21.143
Because they kept it . . .

2061
01:40:21.143 --> 01:40:22.264
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a mirror.

2062
01:40:22.264 --> 01:40:23.758
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, it was beautiful.

2063
01:40:23.758 --> 01:40:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a wooden floor?

2064
01:40:26.000 --> 01:40:27.812
<v HUNTSBERRY>It was a wooden floor.

2065
01:40:27.812 --> 01:40:35.420
And my cousin would get down on . . . Cousin Easter would get on her knees and they would . . . She would wax it.

2066
01:40:35.420 --> 01:40:39.406
She’ll wax it just on her knees she’ll hand wax it.

2067
01:40:39.406 --> 01:40:41.580
And that thing would be pretty.

2068
01:40:41.580 --> 01:40:43.029
It would be beautiful.

2069
01:40:43.029 --> 01:40:44.478
And they did that.

2070
01:40:44.478 --> 01:40:47.014
They kept . . . They had a beautiful house.

2071
01:40:47.014 --> 01:40:48.464
<v ARSENEAULT>Your grandfather built it?

2072
01:40:48.464 --> 01:40:50.275
Or he had it built?

2073
01:40:50.275 --> 01:40:52.812
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah well I think they built it.

2074
01:40:52.812 --> 01:41:00.420
I think Joe said they built it in the time because she was staying somewhere else and they built that house.

2075
01:41:00.420 --> 01:41:05.855
Because she had all them kids and she didn’t want her kids to be separated.

2076
01:41:05.855 --> 01:41:08.029
So they built them a home.

2077
01:41:08.029 --> 01:41:09.841
So . . . but it was beautiful.

2078
01:41:09.841 --> 01:41:12.377
It was the biggest house in Queensboro.

2079
01:41:12.377 --> 01:41:13.464
It was beautiful.

2080
01:41:13.464 --> 01:41:14.551
It was beautiful.

2081
01:41:14.551 --> 01:41:15.275
It was

2082
01:41:15.275 --> 01:41:16.000
<c.p>beautiful.

2083
01:41:16.000 --> 01:41:17.667
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your neighbor’s houses?

2084
01:41:17.667 --> 01:41:19.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Ms. . . . They was wooden.

2085
01:41:19.000 --> 01:41:20.333
They was all wooden.

2086
01:41:20.333 --> 01:41:24.667
Because where my grandma was, behind her, they had a lady Ms. [Rosette?].

2087
01:41:24.667 --> 01:41:32.333
They call her Ms. Rosette and she was blind and I used to say Daddy . . . Daddy would go to the store for her.

2088
01:41:32.333 --> 01:41:35.667
And . . . Give it to him so he can be quiet.

2089
01:41:35.667 --> 01:41:42.000
She was blind, but she’ll take that money . . . she have her money tied into a handkerchief in her bosom.

2090
01:41:42.000 --> 01:41:49.000
And she would take that money and she would take that money and do like this and she would say . . . Five,

2091
01:41:49.000 --> 01:41:53.333
and twenty . . . and I used to go I said, “How she know that?”

2092
01:41:53.333 --> 01:41:54.667
Daddy said she knows.

2093
01:41:54.667 --> 01:41:56.000
But she was blind.

2094
01:41:56.000 --> 01:41:58.667
I said, “Daddy is she really really blind?”

2095
01:41:58.667 --> 01:42:04.000
Because they wouldn’t tell you too much back there, but he would say she was blind.

2096
01:42:04.000 --> 01:42:13.333
But she would do her hand like this with that money and when she come back with the change she would pat the change and she’ll tell you,

2097
01:42:13.333 --> 01:42:14.333
“You shorting me.”

2098
01:42:14.333 --> 01:42:15.667
And he would laugh.

2099
01:42:15.667 --> 01:42:17.333
He’d say, “I told you.

2100
01:42:17.333 --> 01:42:20.000
You can’t get nothing past them old people.”

2101
01:42:20.000 --> 01:42:22.667
They would not put money in a bank.

2102
01:42:22.667 --> 01:42:27.000
They kept they money on them or they hid it on they land.

2103
01:42:27.000 --> 01:42:29.667
They wouldn’t . . . they didn’t believe in the bank.

2104
01:42:29.667 --> 01:42:30.333
They wouldn’t.

2105
01:42:30.333 --> 01:42:31.667
So, but she was . . .

2106
01:42:31.667 --> 01:42:34.000
<v CHILD>They got some water over there.

2107
01:42:34.000 --> 01:42:36.646
<v HUNTSBERRY>Okay I’m going to get it up.

2108
01:42:36.646 --> 01:42:38.159
Don’t worry about it.

2109
01:42:38.159 --> 01:42:40.805
Who else besides you and your brother?

2110
01:42:40.805 --> 01:42:42.695
<v CHILD>Can I have some water?

2111
01:42:42.695 --> 01:42:44.585
<v HUNTSBERRY>Kitchen . . . Go in the kitchen.

2112
01:42:44.585 --> 01:42:46.854
I’m going to give you some.

2113
01:42:46.854 --> 01:42:47.610
Go ahead.

2114
01:42:47.610 --> 01:42:50.634
They did that back then in them days.

2115
01:42:50.634 --> 01:42:54.793
But like I said the neighbor’s house, it was all wooden.

2116
01:42:54.793 --> 01:43:02.354
It was wooden so . . . because when you’ll walk you’ll . . . Them elderly people they took care of they self back then.

2117
01:43:02.354 --> 01:43:05.000
They took care of they self.

2118
01:43:05.000 --> 01:43:11.115
<v ARSENEAULT>Did most people build their own houses back then?

2119
01:43:11.115 --> 01:43:13.154
<v HUNTSBERRY>I don’t know.

2120
01:43:13.154 --> 01:43:15.192
I don’t know.

2121
01:43:15.192 --> 01:43:17.231
I never asked.

2122
01:43:17.231 --> 01:43:23.346
I didn’t ask them. [To child] Don’t do that.

2123
01:43:23.346 --> 01:43:30.821
Go get a towel out the bathroom and wipe that up.

2124
01:43:30.821 --> 01:43:40.333
I never asked them if they built they own homes or what back then.

2125
01:43:40.333 --> 01:43:53.244
But it was a lot of the old people they all gone, dead and gone so . . . Stop that baby.

2126
01:43:53.244 --> 01:43:58.000
But yeah, I didn’t ask them.

2127
01:43:58.000 --> 01:44:02.041
<v ARSENEAULT>So a couple more . . . We’re almost at two hours.

2128
01:44:02.041 --> 01:44:04.286
So a couple more questions.

2129
01:44:04.286 --> 01:44:12.816
What about like going back to the local businesses and stuff, so your mom made most of your clothes?

2130
01:44:12.816 --> 01:44:13.714
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah [agrees].

2131
01:44:13.714 --> 01:44:16.857
<v ARSENEAULT>So she would make her own patterns?

2132
01:44:16.857 --> 01:44:20.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>They didn’t have patterns back then.

2133
01:44:20.000 --> 01:44:20.283
<v ARSENEAULT>Really?

2134
01:44:20.283 --> 01:44:21.978
<v HUNTSBERRY>Momma cut out of her head.

2135
01:44:21.978 --> 01:44:26.217
She can just lay a thing down and just cut and it would be beautiful.

2136
01:44:26.217 --> 01:44:28.761
She taught us how to sew from a pattern.

2137
01:44:28.761 --> 01:44:31.304
Or either she’ll cut it . . . Whatever she think about,

2138
01:44:31.304 --> 01:44:37.239
she would take and draw it on a newspaper and cut the newspaper out and use the newspaper as a pattern.

2139
01:44:37.239 --> 01:44:38.087
She did that.

2140
01:44:38.087 --> 01:44:39.500
<v ARSENEAULT>She didn’t even buy patterns?

2141
01:44:39.500 --> 01:44:48.261
<v HUNTSBERRY>She bought them as we . . . When we got older and was in school because we had to buy them for school because we was . . . at home ec and stuff like that.

2142
01:44:48.261 --> 01:44:51.370
So she would show us how to do it from there.

2143
01:44:51.370 --> 01:44:57.022
But a lot of the . . . it was something that she wants to make, she just do it from her memory.

2144
01:44:57.022 --> 01:44:57.870
She just sewed.

2145
01:44:57.870 --> 01:44:59.848
And she did, and she taught us.

2146
01:44:59.848 --> 01:45:02.391
She even went so far making bras and underclothes.

2147
01:45:02.391 --> 01:45:03.804
She make all of that.

2148
01:45:03.804 --> 01:45:05.217
She did all of that.

2149
01:45:05.217 --> 01:45:06.348
And so that’s it.

2150
01:45:06.348 --> 01:45:10.870
We didn’t . . . we went to the stores to get some shoes or tennis shoes and socks.

2151
01:45:10.870 --> 01:45:12.000
That was it.

2152
01:45:12.000 --> 01:45:13.341
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you go to . . .?

2153
01:45:13.341 --> 01:45:21.390
<v HUNTSBERRY>It was a TJ and Y. We had a store called TJ and Y. And it had everything and it had the . . . They had material they had clothes and stuff.

2154
01:45:21.390 --> 01:45:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it in Lake Charles?

2155
01:45:23.000 --> 01:45:26.691
<v HUNTSBERRY>No, that was in . . . TJ and Y was in Westlake.

2156
01:45:26.691 --> 01:45:31.121
It was in Westlake and then they moved it on Highway 90.

2157
01:45:31.121 --> 01:45:35.181
But that’s where we used to go a lot and do.

2158
01:45:35.181 --> 01:45:42.933
But like I said material-wise, her sister from Houston would send her material and we would make clothes out of that.

2159
01:45:42.933 --> 01:45:45.517
We would make clothes out of that.

2160
01:45:45.517 --> 01:45:46.993
<v ARSENEAULT>What about . . . Be careful.

2161
01:45:46.993 --> 01:45:49.577
Where did you get your hair done?

2162
01:45:49.577 --> 01:45:52.161
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, no hair . . . Momma pressed our hair.

2163
01:45:52.161 --> 01:45:54.376
We . . . Momma took care of that.

2164
01:45:54.376 --> 01:45:58.067
We didn’t . . . Or either we’ll go down to Ms. [Trent?].

2165
01:45:58.067 --> 01:46:01.020
Ms. Trent did a lot of pressing hair.

2166
01:46:01.020 --> 01:46:01.758
Stop, Caden.

2167
01:46:01.758 --> 01:46:02.497
Stop, Caden.

2168
01:46:02.497 --> 01:46:05.450
<v ARSENEAULT>I’m trying to keep him away from this.

2169
01:46:05.450 --> 01:46:08.034
I know he wants that red button.

2170
01:46:08.034 --> 01:46:09.879
I’m going to hide it.

2171
01:46:09.879 --> 01:46:11.356
Hide it from him.

2172
01:46:11.356 --> 01:46:12.094
<v HUNTSBERRY>Caden, stop.

2173
01:46:12.094 --> 01:46:15.785
Yeah but other than that Momma would do our hair.

2174
01:46:15.785 --> 01:46:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How would she press it?

2175
01:46:18.000 --> 01:46:19.200
<v HUNTSBERRY>With a pressing comb.

2176
01:46:19.200 --> 01:46:25.800
We had a pressing comb and she’ll just heat it on the stove and just with the oil and just press it.

2177
01:46:25.800 --> 01:46:27.600
But she took care of that.

2178
01:46:27.600 --> 01:46:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your brother’s haircuts and stuff?

2179
01:46:30.000 --> 01:46:31.304
<v HUNTSBERRY>I don’t know.

2180
01:46:31.304 --> 01:46:34.348
I don’t know who cut they hair.

2181
01:46:34.348 --> 01:46:35.652
I don’t remember.

2182
01:46:35.652 --> 01:46:36.957
I don’t know.

2183
01:46:36.957 --> 01:46:38.696
<v ARSENEAULT>What about special events?

2184
01:46:38.696 --> 01:46:40.435
Y’all do anything special?

2185
01:46:40.435 --> 01:46:40.870
<v HUNTSBERRY>No.

2186
01:46:40.870 --> 01:46:43.913
<v ARSENEAULT>For your hair or anything like that?

2187
01:46:43.913 --> 01:46:44.348
<v HUNTSBERRY>[Disagrees].

2188
01:46:44.348 --> 01:46:45.652
No. nothing special.

2189
01:46:45.652 --> 01:46:50.870
Not us because like I said work [hard?] and stay at home.

2190
01:46:50.870 --> 01:46:52.174
That was us.

2191
01:46:52.174 --> 01:46:53.478
That was us.

2192
01:46:53.478 --> 01:47:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I was just curious if there was like a barber in Mossville or anything.

2193
01:47:00.000 --> 01:47:01.291
<v HUNTSBERRY>A what, baby?

2194
01:47:01.291 --> 01:47:02.152
<v ARSENEAULT>A barber . . .

2195
01:47:02.152 --> 01:47:05.595
<v HUNTSBERRY>Mr. Thibodaux . . . in Bel Air would cut hair.

2196
01:47:05.595 --> 01:47:08.177
He did a lot of cutting.

2197
01:47:08.177 --> 01:47:13.772
I don’t know if Jo Jo and them went to them or what.

2198
01:47:13.772 --> 01:47:23.241
He probably did because Mr. Thibodaux was the only one that cut hair that I remember, but I don’t remember nobody else.

2199
01:47:23.241 --> 01:47:24.962
But Mr. Thibodaux did.

2200
01:47:24.962 --> 01:47:26.253
He cut hair.

2201
01:47:26.253 --> 01:47:28.405
He had his own thing.

2202
01:47:28.405 --> 01:47:32.709
Matter of fact, his daughters and them cut hair now.

2203
01:47:32.709 --> 01:47:33.139
<v ARSENEAULT>Family . . .

2204
01:47:33.139 --> 01:47:34.000
<v HUNTSBERRY>Huh?

2205
01:47:34.000 --> 01:47:35.952
<v ARSENEAULT>Family business or something.

2206
01:47:35.952 --> 01:47:38.880
<v HUNTSBERRY>Yeah they . . . The girls cut hair.

2207
01:47:38.880 --> 01:47:52.544
I don’t . . . know if mister . . . that’s probably . . . if he got his haircut that’s probably where they went because it was the only that I knew at the time.

2208
01:47:52.544 --> 01:47:53.520
Mr. Thibodaux.

2209
01:47:53.520 --> 01:47:54.008
Yeah.

2210
01:47:54.008 --> 01:47:55.472
That was it.

2211
01:47:55.472 --> 01:47:56.936
<v ARSENEAULT>And let’s see.

2212
01:47:56.936 --> 01:47:59.376
Okay, we are almost done.

2213
01:47:59.376 --> 01:48:02.792
So just to kind of wrap up.

2214
01:48:02.792 --> 01:48:07.184
What are some of your fondest memories of Mossville?

2215
01:48:07.184 --> 01:48:11.576
<v HUNTSBERRY>Really is . . . Mossville was a community that stick together.

2216
01:48:11.576 --> 01:48:15.480
We stayed together and like I said quietness.

2217
01:48:15.480 --> 01:48:26.216
And then like I said we played a lot, but other than that like I said I was a home bounded person.

2218
01:48:26.216 --> 01:48:30.120
I just stayed in the house watching T.V.

2219
01:48:30.120 --> 01:48:32.072
<v ARSENEAULT>What would you watch?

2220
01:48:32.072 --> 01:48:35.000
What was your favorite show?

2221
01:48:35.000 --> 01:48:39.856
<v HUNTSBERRY>Oh, honey I would watch . . . The Bonanzas would come on at the time.

2222
01:48:39.856 --> 01:48:41.724
I’d watch them a lot.

2223
01:48:41.724 --> 01:48:44.339
I would watch Elvis Presley a lot.

2224
01:48:44.339 --> 01:48:46.580
Phyllis Dillers and stuff like that.

